thinking_content: '**Analyzing scoring methods**


  I need to focus on achieving a high score at Research Counter and publishing papers. To reach a high score, it looks like I need to implement the best methods that align with a stable state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of around 0.7487–0.7489. Previously, our best score was 0.7458, but it seems the scoring might have shifted. It’s intriguing to see how the previous minimal attempts yield much lower scores around 0.53, indicating potential changes in the scoring system.**Developing a strategy for scoring**


  I''m wondering if those earlier 0.74 results came from a different dataset or environment. My focus is on our own submissions, which all have low scores. It might be a good idea to use the GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB pipeline from our previous work. I see we have several modules, like "best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50_anscombe.py" and "best_sota.py." The goal is to select a high-performing method, keeping in mind that code and dataset configurations might vary. I need to ensure the code adheres to the standardized function signature.**Designing a robust submission**


  I need to create a new submission that utilizes our "best_sota" pipeline while ensuring it has fallbacks for different environments. I''ll explore the "best_sota" and "best_pipeline.py" code, using storage to access them. It''s important to make sure we abide by the specified constraints, like only using batch labels instead of cell_type. I should also check the system/main.py for plugin expectations and review the metrics outlined in openproblems_metrics to properly fill in the required keys. I''ll dive into relevant modules such as praxis/best_sota.py and graphfwpca_ridge.py to confirm the correct paths.**Preparing submission strategy**


  I’m looking into the ''balanced_graph'' and ''daqb'' modules, which define Balanced Reconstruction Balanced Graph. I wonder if the code length might slow us down. From the scoreboard, it appears earlier attempts didn''t score well, possibly due to pbve log1p branches or the GraphFWPCA ridge branch not being listed. We need to submit a robust pipeline like "best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50_anscombe.py" or "best_sota" for a better score. I’ll check out best_sota.py while ensuring we stay within memory and time constraints, aiming for under 30 minutes for 20k cells. We must avoid calling test() to ensure evaluations aren’t overridden.'
