  \ second dataset, 'batch_integration_train.h5ad'. The \"human_train.h5ad\" likely includes multiple organs, but I’m uncertain we can present generalization to \"PBVE baseline\" or \"EQ-Quota\". \n\nThe reviewer expects us to apply it in a second, distinct setting, which could involve different metrics or a frozen baseline dataset.**Planning dataset inspection**\n\nI think we can use \"human_train.h5ad\" as the second dataset, but I need to inspect its content first. Unfortunately, I can't open the file directly and can only access it by reading storage. I might need to execute '/execute_action{storage read system/human_train.h5ad}', though reading binary might just print gibberish. The environment isn't interactive, but I can write a 'test' function to load and compute metrics.\n\nThe plan is to submit to Research Counter and define the 'test' function to run our baseline integration function on the second dataset. I’ll compute metrics and save the log results to STDOUT, even if it shows\
  \ “Score: NaN” because the test returns a string. After that, we can retrieve logs with '/execute_action{review eval_id}'.**Preparing archive submission**\n\nI need to prepare a proper Archive submission, which requires standard sections and detailed methods, along with results for both the Heart dataset and the \"Human multi-organ\" dataset. It’s crucial to get the metrics in the correct order since we received feedback that we reported them incorrectly before. The research task lists the metrics in a specific order, from ASW_batch to Cell_cycle.\n\nIn the paper, I’ll follow that order while removing the \"Next steps\" and \"Reproducibility\" sections. Instead, I’ll include these as part of 'Methods' and 'Implementation details' without referencing local files. I want to provide a self-contained submission with pseudocode and ensure generalization results are present. A table of results for both datasets will be included as well.**Completing metric calculations**\n\nI need actual numbers\
  \ to present, so the test run will produce metric outputs using 'openproblems_metrics.normalize' or 'openproblems_metrics.score'. I'll start by coding the 'test' function to load 'storage/system/human_train.h5ad' with anndata and integrate my function from 'praxis'.\n\nFirst, I'll check the contents of 'openproblems_metrics.py' by executing a storage read. After I've gathered the functions I need, I’ll define 'compute_metrics' to calculate raw and normalized metrics. It's important to prepare a self-contained Archive submission with all necessary sections, including results for both datasets and specified metrics in the correct order. I'll also ensure I avoid any references to local files and unnecessary sections.**Planning for metric generalization**\n\nThe user hasn't requested a new archive yet, but I need to respond to the rejection by planning to revise. Implementing generalization first is key, so I’ll draft the assistant response with necessary actions, such as reading metrics and\
  \ submitting a test.\n\nI’ll create a new draft capsule with a standard structure but placeholders for the second dataset results, while I wait for logs. It’s important for me to adhere to platform constraints, like quoting special characters in YAML, keeping the abstract under 100 words, and including code. \n\nSo, the actions I’ll take are: \n- Execute a storage read for 'openproblems_metrics.py'\n- Go to research\n- Submit the test with the defined title and abstract. \n\nNext, I'll write the code while ensuring I check function signatures by reading the file."
