thinking_content: "**Finalizing archive submission**\n\nI see that reading the metrics file is essential for our work. The archive paper got rejected, and now we need a full paper that includes a generalization test. Once we've completed the test and verified our metrics, I’ll prepare the submission according to the proper format with all necessary sections, including method details. I must remember to include results for both the heart dataset and the second dataset, ensuring we present the metrics clearly. Let's do this systematically!**Organizing metric results**\n\nI'm looking at the metrics results and noticed the order isn't what I expected. The specified research order should be \"ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle,\" but the table displays them differently. To meet the paper requirements, I need to present them in the correct order for the \"order\" check. \n\nNow, I’m also considering which dataset qualifies as the second one. The choices\
  \ seem to be between 'human_train.h5ad' and 'batch_integration_train.h5ad.' I suspect \"human_train.h5ad\" could work, but I’m unsure about the other. Let's clarify this!**Exploring available datasets**\n\nI'm considering running a test on either 'batch_integration_train.h5ad' or 'human_train_solution.h5ad.' Ironically, the 'solution' dataset contains cell types, but I’m allowed not to use those labels locally—just for metrics. It seems like the metrics compute function might compare predictions against the \"solution\" dataset, but it likely uses the cell types for the output.\n\nTo clarify what’s expected, I’ll dig into 'system/openproblems_metrics.py.' I previously encountered issues accessing it, but now that I’m at the Research Counter, I can read it. I'll then adjust my approach accordingly!**Preparing a new submission**\n\nI need to craft a new submission using the test() function with the \"batch_integration_train.h5ad\" dataset as the second one. It's important that the metrics\
  \ are printed in the correct order along with the overall mean. \n\nEarlier, the test function had an \"adhoc\" setting—so I'll need to adjust that. I should also think about updating Private Memory or Meta. After a successful generalization test, I can then create a revised archive submission that includes full sections and results from both the heart and second datasets. But first, I must run the generalization test and gather the necessary metrics to ensure they support verifiable claims.**Updating the revision plan**\n\nI need to set a meta update to keep track of the \"Archive revision plan.\" It's crucial that I keep this update minimal while ensuring I cover all the key aspects. This will help me stay organized as I move forward with crafting the revised submission and managing the related tasks. I'm eager to translate this plan into action, so let's go ahead and produce that update! I've got this!"
