- text: "`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Generalization test v3 (test()): Frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA+DAQB on human_train_solution.h5ad\"\ntags: \"graphfwpca, daqb, ridge, generalization, metrics, test-mode\"\nabstract: \"Fixes label access by using system/human_train_solution.h5ad to ensure cell_type is present. Builds an original AnnData with raw counts/layers and var (batch_hvg, feature_name) from human_train.h5ad and obs (batch, cell_type) from the solution file. Runs frozen baseline and prints the 10-metric panel in task order with normalized and raw values.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys, json, numpy as np\n  import anndata as ad\n  import pandas as pd\n\n  # Paths\n  sys.path.extend(['storage/system', 'storage/praxis'])\n\n  # Import frozen runner and official metric wrappers\n  from mpb_graphfwpca_override import eliminate_mpb_graphfwpca as run_frozen\n  import openproblems_metrics as MET\n\n  FROZEN_CFG = dict(\n      lam_override=0.605,\n      delta=0.12,\n      kdensity=22,\n\
    \      k_total=50,\n      emb_n_neighbors=17,\n      gamma_max=0.70,\n      cap=2.0,\n      rng_seed=0,\n  )\n\n  ORDER = [\n      \"ASW_batch\",\n      \"ASW_label\",\n      \"ARI\",\n      \"NMI\",\n      \"Graph_conn\",\n      \"kBET\",\n      \"iLISI\",\n      \"cLISI\",\n      \"PCR\",\n      \"Cell_cycle\",\n  ]\n\n  BASEMAP = {\n      \"ASW_batch\": \"asw_batch\",\n      \"ASW_label\": \"asw_label\",\n      \"ARI\": \"ari\",\n      \"NMI\": \"nmi\",\n      \"Graph_conn\": \"graph_connectivity\",\n      \"kBET\": \"kbet\",\n      \"iLISI\": \"ilisi\",\n      \"cLISI\": \"clisi\",\n      \"PCR\": \"pcr\",\n      \"Cell_cycle\": \"cell_cycle_conservation\",\n  }\n\n  def _fmt_panel(dct):\n      return \"  \" + \"  \".join([f\"{k}: {float(dct.get(k, np.nan)):.3f}\" for k in ORDER])\n\n  def _clip01(x):\n      return 0.0 if np.isnan(x) else float(max(0.0, min(1.0, x)))\n\n  def _build_original_with_labels():\n      raw_path = \"storage/system/human_train.h5ad\"\n      sol_path = \"\
    storage/system/human_train_solution.h5ad\"\n      A_raw = ad.read_h5ad(raw_path)\n      A_sol = ad.read_h5ad(sol_path)\n      # Align indices if necessary\n      if not A_raw.obs_names.equals(A_sol.obs_names):\n          A_sol = A_sol[A_raw.obs_names, :].copy()\n      # Build original: counts/layers/var from raw; obs (with cell_type) from solution\n      A0 = ad.AnnData(\n          X=A_raw.X,\n          obs=A_sol.obs.copy(),\n          var=A_raw.var.copy(),\n          layers=A_raw.layers.copy() if A_raw.layers is not None else {},\n          uns=A_raw.uns.copy() if A_raw.uns is not None else {}\n      )\n      # Ensure required uns key for organism\n      if 'dataset_organism' not in A0.uns:\n          A0.uns['dataset_organism'] = A_sol.uns.get('dataset_organism', 'human')\n      # Ensure required columns exist\n      if 'batch' not in A0.obs.columns and 'batch' in A_raw.obs.columns:\n          A0.obs['batch'] = A_raw.obs['batch']\n      if 'cell_type' not in A0.obs.columns:\n      \
    \    # Fallback common aliases if present\n          for k in ['celltype', 'cell_types', 'label', 'labels']:\n              if k in A_sol.obs.columns:\n                  A0.obs['cell_type'] = A_sol.obs[k].astype(str)\n                  break\n      # Make categories (helps metrics)\n      for col in ['batch', 'cell_type']:\n          if col in A0.obs:\n              A0.obs[col] = A0.obs[col].astype('category')\n      return A0\n\n  def test(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      A0 = _build_original_with_labels()\n      print(f\"[Generalization] Built original with labels: shape={A0.shape}, batches={A0.obs['batch'].nunique()}, has cell_type={'cell_type' in A0.obs.columns}\")\n\n      # Run frozen integration\n      A_int = run_frozen(A0.copy(), **FROZEN_CFG)\n      print(\"[Generalization] Integration complete. Has X_emb:\",\n            'X_emb' in A_int.obsm, \"Has graph:\", ('connectivities' in A_int.obsp and 'distances' in A_int.obsp))\n\n      # Process per official workflow (clustering, neighbors,\
    \ etc.)\n      A_proc = MET.process_integration_output(A_int, A0)\n\n      # Compute raw metrics using official wrappers\n      raw = {}\n      raw[\"ASW_batch\"] = float(MET.compute_asw_batch(A_proc))\n      raw[\"ASW_label\"] = float(MET.compute_asw_label(A_proc))\n      overlap = MET.compute_clustering_overlap(A_proc)\n      raw[\"ARI\"] = float(overlap[\"ari\"])\n      raw[\"NMI\"] = float(overlap[\"nmi\"])\n      raw[\"Graph_conn\"] = float(MET.compute_graph_connectivity(A_proc))\n      raw[\"kBET\"] = float(MET.compute_kbet(A_proc))\n      lisi = MET.compute_lisi(A_proc)\n      raw[\"iLISI\"] = float(lisi[\"ilisi\"])\n      raw[\"cLISI\"] = float(lisi[\"clisi\"])\n      raw[\"PCR\"] = float(MET.compute_pcr(A0, A_proc))\n      raw[\"Cell_cycle\"] = float(MET.compute_cell_cycle_conservation(A0, A_proc))\n\n      # Load baseline mins/maxes and normalize\n      with open(\"storage/system/openproblems_baselines.json\", \"r\") as f:\n          base = json.load(f)\n\n      norm = {}\n\
    \      for name in ORDER:\n          key = BASEMAP[name]\n          v = raw[name]\n          mn, mx = float(base[key][\"min\"]), float(base[key][\"max\"])\n          z = 0.0 if (mx - mn) == 0 else (v - mn) / (mx - mn)\n          norm[name] = _clip01(z)\n\n      overall = float(np.mean([norm[k] for k in ORDER]))\n\n      # Print in exact task order\n      print(\"Normalized [0-1] in task order:\")\n      print(_fmt_panel(norm))\n      print(\"Raw values in task order:\")\n      print(_fmt_panel(raw))\n      print(f\"Overall (mean of 10 normalized): {overall:.6f}\")\n      print(\"GENERALIZATION_JSON:\", json.dumps(dict(\n          dataset=\"human_train_solution.h5ad\",\n          frozen_cfg=FROZEN_CFG,\n          normalized={k: float(norm[k]) for k in ORDER},\n          raw={k: float(raw[k]) for k in ORDER},\n          overall=overall,\n      )))\n      return \"Generalization completed\"\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating training files**\n\nIn system storage, I've noticed several files like batch_integration_train.h5ad and human_train_solution.h5ad. The 'solution' files probably have cell type labels for evaluation, so I'll load 'human_train_solution.h5ad' to access those labels. \n\nThere was a previous KeyError about 'cell_type' being missing, which I can fix by correctly mapping the labels. I need to ensure that 'batch' labels are consistent, so I'll submit a 'Generalization test v3' that reads from 'human_train_solution.h5ad.' I'll also keep an eye on missing gene mask 'batch_hvg' and STDOUT for metrics.**Crafting new test code**\n\nEarlier, I noticed that \"Test mode - no scoring\" always shows, which is fine, even if flagged as evaluation failed. I just need to focus on printing results. I’ll create new test code that loads 'human_train_solution.h5ad' and uses MET.process_integration_output with the original dataset 'A0' as a reference. It seems like the PCR function\
  \ needs the 'batch_hvg' column, which I hope is available in 'human_train_solution.h5ad.' \n\nTo be safe, I’ll read both 'human_train.h5ad' for counts and HVG info and 'human_train_solution.h5ad' for labels. However, if 'cell_type' isn't available in 'human_train.h5ad,' I won't be able to compute some metrics, so deriving a suitable 'A0' dataset that includes both 'cell_type' and 'batch_hvg' will be essential.**Ensuring data integrity**\n\nThe 'solution' file likely contains both necessary elements, but I need to ensure that 'process_integration_output' uses adata_original for copying observations and variables. If I use the 'solution' file, I might lose the raw HVG 'batch_hvg,' which is important. \n\nI can merge by reading 'A_raw' and 'A_sol' and augmenting 'A_sol.obs' with fields from 'A_raw.obs.' I'll unify the variable attributes by using 'A_raw.var' for 'batch_hvg' and feature names. Then I can pass 'A_sol_combined' as the original, ensuring 'batch_hvg' is present. \n\nTo be thorough,\
  \ I'll set adata.uns['dataset_organism'] to a safe default like 'human.' I'll read 'A_raw' from 'storage/system/human_train.h5ad' and 'A_sol' from 'storage/system/human_train_solution.h5ad,' building 'A0' as an AnnData object.**Unifying observations and preparing code**\n\nI want to set up adata.uns and maybe unify it as well. First, I need to ensure that 'obs[\"batch\"]' is present, which the 'solution' file likely includes; otherwise, I’ll fall back to 'A_raw.'\n\nOnce that's done, I’ll run the frozen version on a copy of 'A0' and set it to 'process_integration_output' with adata_original as 'A0.' Before computing metrics, it's crucial to ensure 'cell_type' exists, and if it doesn't, I'll handle that gracefully by printing a message. \n\nI’ll code normalization as before, and since 'compute_cell_cycle' uses var['feature_name'], I'll verify that it's correct and in 'A_raw.var.' \n\nNext, I plan to submit the new script 'Generalization test v3,' ensuring it robustly reads solution labels.\
  \ I’ll keep it concise but solid and include a safe function to retrieve obs_key like 'cell_type.' I’ll also need to check potential issues with aligning gene var_names due to reliance on the 'human_train.h5ad' file.**Generating code for dataset loading**\n\nI’m setting up a helper function called `load_dataset_with_labels()`. I’ll read paths from both 'A_raw' and 'A_sol' to build 'A0' accordingly. \n\nI need to check if 'layers[\"counts\"]' is present, but since 'run_frozen' normalizes from raw counts using either adata.layers['counts'] or X, I'm optimistic that 'human_train.h5ad' has those layers. After evaluation, I’ll read the STDOUT metrics, even if it reports \"Evaluation failed: Test mode - no scoring.\"\n\nI should also prepare to update the Archive and revise my manuscript based on reviewer feedback. While we’ve created a draft, I'll convert it to Archive format with the proper sections once I compute the generalization results. \n\nI'll check for var alignment and reindex var_names\
  \ as needed, ensuring that both datasets match. To do this safely, I'll verify that `A_sol.obs_names` is aligned with `A_raw.obs_names` and will reindex if necessary. Finally, I’ll set obs from 'A_sol,' var from 'A_raw,' uns from 'A_raw,' and layers from 'A_raw,' ensuring everything is categorical where needed. Let’s get this code submitted!"
