tick: 1325
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Graph diagnostics (test()): Frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA+DAQB on Heart (degree, batch‑mixing, σ_kd coupling)\"\ntags: \"diagnostics, graph, mixing, sigma_kd, reproducibility, test-mode\"\nabstract: \"Runs the frozen baseline on the Heart dataset (test mode) and prints graph diagnostics: degree stats, per-cell batch‑mixing fractions, σ_kd summaries (kd=22), and coupling (Pearson/Spearman) between σ_kd and mixing. Outputs JSON blocks for archival.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys, json, numpy as np\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scipy.sparse as sp\n  from scipy.stats import pearsonr, spearmanr\n\n  sys.path.extend(['storage/praxis'])\n  from mpb_graphfwpca_override import eliminate_mpb_graphfwpca as run_frozen\n  from diag_sigma_kd import sigma_kd_from_embedding, global_summary, per_batch_summary\n\n  FROZEN_CFG = dict(\n      lam_override=0.605,\n      delta=0.12,\n      kdensity=22,\n      k_total=50,\n  \
    \    emb_n_neighbors=17,\n      gamma_max=0.70,\n      cap=2.0,\n      rng_seed=0,\n  )\n\n  def _degree_stats(C):\n      deg = np.asarray(C.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\n      return {\n          \"n\": int(deg.size),\n          \"mean\": float(deg.mean()),\n          \"median\": float(np.median(deg)),\n          \"min\": float(deg.min()) if deg.size else 0.0,\n          \"max\": float(deg.max()) if deg.size else 0.0,\n          \"std\": float(deg.std()),\n      }, deg\n\n  def _per_cell_mixing(C, batches):\n      # mixing_other = fraction of neighbors with batch != own batch\n      n = C.shape[0]\n      codes = batches.cat.codes.to_numpy()\n      mixing = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.float32)\n      for i in range(n):\n          start, end = C.indptr[i], C.indptr[i+1]\n          nbrs = C.indices[start:end]\n          d = end - start\n          if d == 0:\n              mixing[i] = np.nan\n          else:\n              mixing[i] = 1.0 - (np.sum(codes[nbrs] == codes[i]) / d)\n      return mixing\n\n\
    \  def _per_batch_summary(vals, batches):\n      out = {}\n      for cat in batches.cat.categories:\n          mask = (batches == cat).to_numpy()\n          v = vals[mask]\n          v = v[~np.isnan(v)]\n          if v.size:\n              out[str(cat)] = {\n                  \"n\": int(v.size),\n                  \"mean\": float(np.mean(v)),\n                  \"median\": float(np.median(v)),\n                  \"std\": float(np.std(v)),\n              }\n      return out\n\n  def test(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      # Run frozen integration on provided Heart data\n      A = run_frozen(adata.copy(), **FROZEN_CFG)\n      print(\"[DIAG] Integration done. Has graph:\", ('connectivities' in A.obsp and 'distances' in A.obsp), \"Has X_emb:\", ('X_emb' in A.obsm))\n\n      # Ensure CSR graph\n      C = A.obsp['connectivities'].tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n      b = A.obs['batch'].astype('category')\n\n      # Degree statistics\n      deg_stats, deg_vec = _degree_stats(C)\n      print(\"DEGREE_JSON:\"\
    , json.dumps(deg_stats))\n\n      # Per-cell batch-mixing\n      mix = _per_cell_mixing(C, b)\n      mix_all = mix[~np.isnan(mix)]\n      mix_summary = {\n          \"n\": int(mix_all.size),\n          \"mean\": float(np.mean(mix_all)),\n          \"median\": float(np.median(mix_all)),\n          \"std\": float(np.std(mix_all)),\n      }\n      mix_by_batch = _per_batch_summary(mix, b)\n      print(\"MIXING_JSON:\", json.dumps({\"all\": mix_summary, \"by_batch\": mix_by_batch}))\n\n      # σ_kd (kd=22) from embedding; summaries\n      sig = sigma_kd_from_embedding(np.asarray(A.obsm['X_emb']), kd=22, extra=16, metric=\"euclidean\")\n      sigma_global = global_summary(sig)\n      sigma_batches = per_batch_summary(sig, b.to_numpy())\n      print(\"SIGMA_GLOBAL_JSON:\", json.dumps(sigma_global))\n      # Convert per-batch dataframe to dict\n      sigma_batches_dict = {str(r[\"batch\"]): {k: r[k] for k in [\"n\",\"median\",\"mean\",\"cv\",\"gini\"]} for _, r in sigma_batches.iterrows()}\n\
    \      print(\"SIGMA_BY_BATCH_JSON:\", json.dumps(sigma_batches_dict))\n\n      # Coupling: σ_kd vs mixing_other (exclude NaNs)\n      msk = ~np.isnan(mix)\n      pr, pp = pearsonr(sig[msk], mix[msk])\n      sr, spv = spearmanr(sig[msk], mix[msk])\n      coupling = {\"pearson_r\": float(pr), \"pearson_p\": float(pp), \"spearman_r\": float(sr), \"spearman_p\": float(spv)}\n      print(\"COUPLING_JSON:\", json.dumps(coupling))\n\n      return \"Graph diagnostics completed\"\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 127}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Publication accepted (Archive #56, 10/10). Reviewer suggested applying graph diagnostics to the frozen baseline.\n  Actions:\n  - Submitted Heart diagnostics (test‑mode) with degree stats, per‑cell batch‑mixing, σ_kd summaries, and σ_kd↔mixing coupling. Will record EID and paste DEGREE_JSON/MIXING_JSON/SIGMA_*_JSON/COUPLING_JSON into Appendix once available.\n  - Baselines EIDs added: 1827 (PCA60+kNN) 0.5332; 1828\
    \ (ComBat→PCA60+kNN) 0.6496.\n  - Generalization panel already logged under EID 1823 (test‑mode).\n  Next: append diagnostics to Archive #56 as Appendix A with EID link.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating diagnostic tests**\n\nI can run code through the Research Counter for the SC dataset, implementing a test function to compute graph diagnostics like inbound degree distribution and per-cell batch mixing. I’ll print summary numbers and maybe save them in graph_diagnostics.py. Since we work with integrated outputs, I’ll also set up a test harness that reads our training data and computes diagnostics. The token budget is somewhat tight, but running in \"test mode\" helps generalization. The results will stay internal.**Updating diagnostic protocols**\n\nI could update the archive by adding diagnostic results that summarize global and per-batch mixing. However, there's a restriction mentioning that we should only include verifiable claims supported by experimental data. I’ll produce actual numbers from the test's output, but we haven’t run it yet. Alternatively, I can create a reproducible diagnostic protocol section without claiming results. Also, I might consider\
  \ adding an appendix for diagnostic methods and preliminary graph statistics once accepted, but we’ll need an Evaluation ID for submission.**Planning diagnostic submission**\n\nI can create a \"Diagnostic test: baseline graph properties on Heart via test()\" submission and generate output with necessary numbers. Once I have those, I'll update the archive with the numbers and our Evaluation ID (EID). My plan is to submit the test with code to compute diagnostics, like per-cell neighbor batch mixing fraction and inbound degree distribution. \n\nTo calculate degree distribution, I'll sum connectivities, and for mixing fractions, I’ll analyze the share of neighbors from different batches. Lastly, I’ll look into the correlation between sigma_kd and mixing fraction for density-mixing coupling.**Setting up diagnostics**\n\nI need to utilize sigma_kd, which is accessed through a function in diag_sigma_kd.py. To import it, I can update the system path and then import the necessary function. In\
  \ test mode, I load the dataset from human_train.h5ad while the evaluation works with the dataset we provide to the test function.\n\nFor the heart dataset, I can integrate the baseline by calling our baseline function on the adata argument, creating a copy. This approach avoids loading files and variable mismatches. I’ll also compute metrics like inbound entropy and density-mixing coupling based on the diagnostic tools.**Preparing diagnostic submission**\n\nI don't have the code for inbound entropy, but I can compute the mix ratio and Gini or CV of sigma_kd by batch using my diagnostic code. I'll submit a test for \"Graph diagnostics (test()): Frozen baseline on Heart,\" including the global summary, per-batch summary, and graph degree stats. \n\nI plan to compute the mixing fraction, correlations using Spearman and Pearson, and extract connectivities for detailed statistics. I'll carefully structure the YAML content and ensure all necessary imports and computations are in place before\
  \ submitting the test run and updating the archive with our results.**Preparing to compute coupling**\n\nTo compute the coupling between sigma and mixing, I need the sigma array. I can obtain this by calling the sigma_kd_from_embedding function, which is great! Now, it's time to write the code for it. \n\nAdditionally, I should include an acceptance message in private memory to keep track of our progress. I'll organize the actions and submit them accordingly to ensure everything is documented and ready for the next steps."
