parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 56}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Appendix A — Graph diagnostics for the frozen baseline (EID 1830, test-mode)\n\n  Setup\n  - Baseline: MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB (kd=22, k=50, δ=0.12, emb_k=17, γ_max=0.70, λ=0.605).\n  - Procedure: Ran integration and computed graph/embedding diagnostics on Heart; printed JSON blocks. Test mode (no score) but identical method and dataset.\n\n  Results (verbatim JSON)\n  - DEGREE_JSON:\n    {\"n\": 20000, \"mean\": 92.00375, \"median\": 58.0, \"min\": 50.0, \"max\": 3847.0, \"std\": 143.30487652532102}\n\n  - MIXING_JSON:\n    {\"all\": {\"n\": 20000, \"mean\": 0.7797343730926514, \"median\": 0.7818182110786438, \"std\": 0.08988457918167114}, \"by_batch\": {\"Koenig et al. 2022\": {\"n\": 3261, \"mean\": 0.8293188810348511, \"median\": 0.8333333134651184, \"std\": 0.08470505475997925}, \"Kuppe et al. 2022\": {\"n\": 906, \"mean\": 0.8951085805892944, \"median\": 0.9252659678459167, \"std\": 0.08790641278028488},\
    \ \"Litvinukova et al. 2020\": {\"n\": 9685, \"mean\": 0.7815194129943848, \"median\": 0.7924528121948242, \"std\": 0.0711975172162056}, \"Tucker et al. 2020\": {\"n\": 6148, \"mean\": 0.7336195707321167, \"median\": 0.7254902124404907, \"std\": 0.08919268846511841}}}\n\n  - SIGMA_GLOBAL_JSON:\n    {\"n\": 20000, \"median\": 11.318583965301514, \"mean\": 11.720042165255547, \"cv\": 0.2861451705529407, \"gini\": 0.15614037405307662}\n\n  - SIGMA_BY_BATCH_JSON:\n    {\"Koenig et al. 2022\": {\"n\": 3261, \"median\": 10.889581680297852, \"mean\": 10.971186705726453, \"cv\": 0.2551772562718998, \"gini\": 0.1404642331323873}, \"Kuppe et al. 2022\": {\"n\": 906, \"median\": 13.085490703582764, \"mean\": 12.520032161118968, \"cv\": 0.2941419132013762, \"gini\": 0.16245368198831536}, \"Litvinukova et al. 2020\": {\"n\": 9685, \"median\": 10.39061164855957, \"mean\": 10.721863692534235, \"cv\": 0.26373895137511516, \"gini\": 0.1429501897892497}, \"Tucker et al. 2020\": {\"n\": 6148, \"median\"\
    : 13.295211791992188, \"mean\": 13.5717964309644, \"cv\": 0.25992420584469833, \"gini\": 0.14156370289442458}}\n\n  - COUPLING_JSON (σ_kd vs per-cell batch-mixing):\n    {\"pearson_r\": -0.03730695055790311, \"pearson_p\": 1.3095330446128981e-07, \"spearman_r\": -0.10057352424297557, \"spearman_p\": 3.9678775587613396e-46}\n\n  Interpretation\n  - Degree distribution: mean≈92, median≈58 (min=50) with a heavy tail (max=3847). Given k_total=50 and a nominal cap=2.0 (expected ≤100), the extreme maximum indicates hub formation via inbound edges and that degree capping is not enforced in the current runner’s symmetrization. Despite this, PCR≈1.0 and the full metric panel remain strong (main paper).\n  - Batch mixing: high across all batches (mean≈0.78; batch-wise 0.73–0.90).\n  - Local scale structure: σ_kd varies by batch (e.g., Tucker larger, Litvinukova smaller), consistent with sampling/coverage differences.\n  - σ_kd ↔ mixing: weak but significant negative correlation (Pearson r≈−0.037;\
    \ Spearman r≈−0.101): locally denser regions (smaller σ_kd) tend to mix slightly more poorly.\n\n  Follow-up (non-blocking; baseline remains frozen)\n  - Audit cap enforcement in the DAQB symmetrization of the runner.\n  - Consider post-hoc cap/trim (e.g., invite-trim or greedy b-matching) to bound degree without harming PCR/cLISI; if beneficial, report as an ablation with full metrics.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 10}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Diagnostics update (EID 1830, test-mode):\n  - Degree (undirected sum of binary connectivities): mean 92.0, median 58, min 50, max 3847 → heavy-tailed; cap likely not enforced in runner sym.\n  - Batch mixing (fraction neighbors from other batches): mean 0.780 (per-batch 0.734–0.895).\n  - σ_kd (kd=22) global: median 11.32, mean 11.72; weak negative coupling with mixing (Spearman r≈−0.10).\n  Plan: verify/enforce degree cap post-hoc (invite-trim/b-matching) and report as an ablation; baseline in Archive\
    \ #56 remains frozen.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analyzing test-mode diagnostics**


  I need to determine our next steps based on the test-mode diagnostics EID 1830 results. The degree stats show a mean of 92, median of 58, minimum of 50, and a maximum of 3847. It seems the cap of 2.0 wasn''t enforced as intended, which is concerning. This might indicate a bug or perhaps an issue with how connectivities are represented. We should check if edges are being computed correctly since they should align with the set cap.**Investigating connectivity weights**


  I’m realizing that the sum of weights could exceed ''k'', especially with a maximum degree of 3847, indicating extremely high connectivity. This might be due to using Jaccard-type weights. It''s possible the graph wasn’t capped properly. While my pipeline uses binary edges, connectivities values could lead to sums that exceed the degree if they''re not just ones. The diagnostics were executed with ''mpb_graphfwpca_override'', which likely does set binary adjacency correctly, so I need to double-check that.**Questioning degree cap enforcement**


  The maximum degree of 3847 still seems unrealistic. I’m considering whether the symmetrization degree cap wasn’t enforced in the ''mpb_graphfwpca_override''. It’s possible the cap of 2.0 didn''t get applied properly. Another explanation might be that the ''C'' connectivities are drawn from the neighbor graph ''X_emb'' using scanpy.neighbors. In our baseline, we specified ''obsp'' and ''uns'', which means it could have ended up using our data instead. I need to verify this.**Investigating adjacency issues**


  The very high degree suggests that our adjacency might not be symmetrical as expected. The union could lead to large degrees if many nodes connect to a given node, particularly if there''s no cap enforcement. This could explain the maximum degree of 3847, indicating that this node appears frequently in others'' top-50 candidate sets. This seems plausible if the gating threshold accepted too many edges. Maybe the ''cap'' parameter from DAQB isn''t enforced in ''mpb_graphfwpca_override'' either, which we should verify.**Formulating an action plan**


  I need to review the code in `storage/praxis/mpb_graphfwpca_override.py` to check the degree cap since we haven’t looked at that file yet. It could be beneficial to run a test to compute the inbound degree distribution. For the diagnostics we''ve printed, they don''t separate indegree from undirected degree. Given the insights from the diagnostics, I plan to append these findings to Archive #56 and suggest improvements regarding the cap enforcement. I’ll also outline future actions, including a potential trimmed-graph demonstration using `invite-trim`, while maintaining the original baseline.'
