role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{review 1833}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Balanced invite-trim (cap=100, keep cross-batch) after DAQB sym: MPB‑GraphFWPCA+DAQB ridge\"\ntags: \"graphfwpca, daqb, degree-cap, trimming, reproducibility, ablation\"\nabstract: \"Naive cap=100 trimming hurt iLISI by removing long cross-batch bridges. This ablation enforces cap=100 but prioritizes removing same-batch, long-distance edges first and respects a small degree floor, aiming to preserve cross-batch mixing while bounding hubs.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scipy.sparse as sp\n\n  sys.path.extend(['storage', 'storage/praxis'])\n  from mpb_graphfwpca_override import eliminate_mpb_graphfwpca as run_frozen\n\n  def _deg_stats(C):\n      deg = np.asarray(C.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\n      return (float(deg.min()), float(np.median(deg)), float(deg.mean()), float(deg.max()))\n\n  def _invite_trim_cap_balanced(C:\
    \ sp.csr_matrix, D: sp.csr_matrix, batches: np.ndarray, cap_deg: int, deg_floor: int = 40):\n      \"\"\"\n      Enforce max undirected degree <= cap_deg by removing edges greedily with priority:\n        1) same-batch edges first\n        2) larger distances first\n      Skip removals that would drop either endpoint below deg_floor.\n      Works on symmetric binary C and symmetric D (min distances).\n      \"\"\"\n      n = C.shape[0]\n      Cu = sp.triu(C, k=1).tocoo()\n      Du = sp.triu(D, k=1).tocoo()\n      rows, cols = Cu.row, Cu.col\n\n      # Distances aligned to structure (fallback mapping if mismatched)\n      if Du.nnz != Cu.nnz or not (np.array_equal(Du.row, rows) and np.array_equal(Du.col, cols)):\n          key = Du.row.astype(np.int64) * n + Du.col.astype(np.int64)\n          dmap = dict(zip(key.tolist(), Du.data.tolist()))\n          w = np.array([dmap.get(int(r)*n + int(c), np.inf) for r, c in zip(rows, cols)], dtype=np.float64)\n      else:\n          w = Du.data.astype(np.float64)\n\
    \n      # Batch same/diff mask for upper-tri edges\n      # batches should be categorical codes or strings aligned with nodes\n      b = batches\n      if hasattr(b, \"cat\"):\n          codes = b.cat.codes.to_numpy()\n      else:\n          # map strings to codes\n          _, inv = np.unique(b.astype(str), return_inverse=True)\n          codes = inv\n      same = (codes[rows] == codes[cols])\n\n      # Priority score: remove same-batch edges first, then by larger distance\n      # Use a combined priority value: same -> +1e6, plus distance\n      pri = same.astype(np.int64) * 1_000_000 + w\n      order = np.argsort(pri)[::-1]\n\n      # Degrees from undirected graph\n      deg = np.asarray(C.sum(axis=1)).ravel().astype(np.int64)\n\n      keep = np.ones(rows.size, dtype=bool)\n      for idx in order:\n          i = rows[idx]; j = cols[idx]\n          if deg[i] > cap_deg or deg[j] > cap_deg:\n              # Avoid collapsing endpoints below a small floor\n              if deg[i] <= deg_floor\
    \ or deg[j] <= deg_floor:\n                  continue\n              keep[idx] = False\n              deg[i] -= 1\n              deg[j] -= 1\n\n      # Rebuild symmetric graphs from kept edges\n      kr, kc, kw = rows[keep], cols[keep], w[keep]\n      ii = np.concatenate([kr, kc]); jj = np.concatenate([kc, kr])\n      C_new = sp.coo_matrix((np.ones(ii.size, dtype=np.float64), (ii, jj)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      D_new = sp.coo_matrix((np.concatenate([kw, kw]).astype(np.float64), (ii, jj)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      C_new.sort_indices(); D_new.sort_indices()\n      return C_new, D_new\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # 1) Run frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB ridge (kd=22, k=50, δ=0.12, emb_k=17, γ_max=0.70, λ=0.605)\n      A = run_frozen(\n          adata,\n          lam_override=0.605, delta=0.12, kdensity=22, k_total=50,\n          emb_n_neighbors=17, gamma_max=0.70, cap=2.0, rng_seed=0\n      )\n\n      # 2) Balanced invite-trim with cap=100,\
    \ prefer removing same-batch edges, enforce floor\n      C = A.obsp['connectivities'].tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n      D = A.obsp['distances'].tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n      batches = A.obs['batch']\n\n      pre = _deg_stats(C)\n      print(f\"[BALCAP100] degree pre (min,med,mean,max) = {pre}\")\n      C2, D2 = _invite_trim_cap_balanced(C, D, batches=np.asarray(batches).astype(str), cap_deg=100, deg_floor=40)\n      post = _deg_stats(C2)\n      print(f\"[BALCAP100] degree post (min,med,mean,max) = {post}\")\n\n      A.obsp['connectivities'] = C2\n      A.obsp['distances'] = D2\n      if 'neighbors' not in A.uns:\n          A.uns['neighbors'] = {}\n      A.uns['neighbors']['connectivities_key'] = 'connectivities'\n      A.uns['neighbors']['distances_key'] = 'distances'\n      params = A.uns['neighbors'].get('params', {})\n      params.update({'degree_cap': 100, 'postprocess': 'invite_trim_balanced_same_first', 'deg_floor': 40})\n      A.uns['neighbors']['params'] = params\n      return\
    \ A\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 127}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Degree-cap ablation — first result:\n  - 1831 cap=100 (naive distance trim) → 0.6973; iLISI collapsed to 0.470 while ARI/NMI increased; confirms that removing long edges indiscriminately harms mixing.\n  - Submitted balanced cap=100 (same-batch-first, deg_floor=40) to preserve cross-batch bridges; 1833 (cap=80 naive) is running; cap=120 was queued for later.\n  Plan: keep baseline frozen; report cap variants as negative/neutral unless a balanced cap improves ≥+0.002 overall with PCR≈1.0 and stable cLISI.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Updating archive strategy**\n\nI need to record the results and update our archives and public memory. I'm considering revised trimming strategies. One idea is to prioritize removing edges that belong to the same batch, which helps maintain cross-batch mixing. We could also drop non-reciprocal edges first instead of focusing on distance. Additionally, it might make sense to compute a cap on inbound edges for each node, ensuring a balanced degree capping to maintain equality across batches. It seems a bit complex!**Implementing post-processing strategy**\n\nI’m thinking about a post-process we can use. First, for each node, if the degree exceeds the cap, I can divide neighbor edges into groups based on whether they're from the same-batch or different-batch. Then, I would compute distances and prioritize removing edges, starting with the longest same-batch edges. I must ensure we keep at least one neighbor from each batch when possible, and I see that using batch labels\
  \ for integration is indeed allowed. It’s a bit complicated, but I think I can manage!**Planning submission and adjustments**\n\nI’m thinking about coding a new submission for \"Invite-trim cap balanced by batch,\" aiming to preserve cross-batch edges. I’m considering a cap of either 100 or 80, but since 100 caused a major drop, I’ll start with the balanced trimming approach. I want to ensure that we manage Graph_conn and update neighbor parameters. I’ll print the degree stats and mixing to verify results. For now, I should review task 1833 and record results in memory once it’s complete. I'll also consider pruning tokens if we get close to 75%.**Planning next actions**\n\nI’m mapping out the next steps. First, I need to go to research and review task 1833 to see if it’s still running. If it’s pending, I can’t submit anything new but can prepare the code in storage to save on token usage. We should submit the balanced cap for degree 100 when there’s available concurrency. Since 1831 is\
  \ finished and only 1833 is running, I can submit the new 1834 now. After the run, I’ll compute for nodes exceeding the cap and decide on edges to drop, using the symmetric binary CSR and keeping an eye on the upper triangle to avoid duplicating efforts.**Developing edge removal strategy**\n\nI need to incorporate batch labels into our edge removal process, prioritizing the removal of same-batch edges first. For each edge (i, j), I'll create a tuple containing a flag for if it’s a same-batch edge and the distance. I’ll sort the edges based on this priority: first same-batch edges with the largest distances. \n\nFor nodes exceeding their degree cap, we will drop edges involving those nodes. Although sorting edges globally could complicate things, I think implementing a greedy approach will help. I also want to ensure we don’t drop a node’s degree below a specified minimum and maintain the integrity of connections while working through the edges. \n\nUltimately, I plan to submit the balanced\
  \ cap version and print degree changes along with a summary. Once ready, I’ll prepare the code for submission and also update our archives with results. I’ll go to research next to review task 1833 and possibly submit our new variant.**Implementing the balanced trimming function**\n\nI’m going to implement the function `_invite_trim_cap_balanced` that works with rows, columns, and weights. I’ll create a mask to identify same-batch edges and compute a priority score to sort the edges. The priority will consider same-batch flags and weights. \n\nNext, while removing edges, I'll check conditions to ensure both endpoints exceed the degree floor; this should help preserve low-degree nodes while allowing for trims on high-degree hubs. \n\nOnce I finalize the code, I’ll move on to research, review task 1833, and submit the Balanced variant. I’ll also update memory and possibly the archive later. Let’s get started on crafting the code!"
