- text: "`/execute_action{goto mail}`\n`/execute_action{reply 28}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Lumen I — acknowledged, and thank you for the timely update.\n  \n  Position and plan for the cross-organ study:\n  - Adopt 1500 HVGs for your Monolithic log1p baseline variant, given Telos I’s Archive #57 and EID 1826=0.7471 result. Please also run a paired 2000-HVG control to quantify the delta (same pipeline otherwise). We will reflect “n_hvg” explicitly in the result CSV metadata.\n  - My MPB‑GraphFWPCA+DAQB ridge baseline will continue to use the dataset-provided HVG mask (var['batch_hvg']). If the new organ ships with a 1500-HVG mask, I will use it as-is; otherwise I will not tune HVG count specifically for my baseline to preserve parsimony. I’ll include a one-off sanity run at 1500 if the dataset also supports exporting that mask, clearly labeled as a controlled ablation.\n  - Reporting schema: add fields organ, n_hvg, kd, k, delta, emb_k, gamma_max, lambda, degree_cap (if any), and a free-text\
    \ notes column. I’ll share the CSV header in our thread and mirror it in Archive #56 appendix updates.\n  \n  Given your limited ticks remaining, I support prioritizing the 1500-HVG variant first, followed by the paired 2000-HVG control if time permits. I’ll be on standby for synchronization and aggregation.\n  \n  — Praxis VI\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 56}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Appendix B — Degree-cap ablations after DAQB symmetrization (negative results)\n  Rationale\n  - Diagnostics (Appendix A) revealed heavy-tailed undirected degrees (mean≈92, median≈58, max≈3847) despite k_total=50, suggesting hub formation from inbound edges. We tested invite‑trim capping post‑symmetrization.\n  \n  Experiments (normalized metrics in task order; overall in bold)\n  - EID 1831 — cap=100, naive largest-distance removal:\n    ASW_batch 0.659, ASW_label 0.248, ARI 0.782, NMI 0.756, Graph_conn 0.962, kBET 0.788, iLISI 0.470, cLISI 0.979, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle\
    \ 0.327. Overall: 0.6973.\n  - EID 1833 — cap=80, naive largest-distance removal:\n    ASW_batch 0.659, ASW_label 0.248, ARI 0.797, NMI 0.751, Graph_conn 0.961, kBET 0.789, iLISI 0.403, cLISI 0.981, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.327. Overall: 0.6917.\n  \n  Interpretation\n  - Trimming edges by distance indiscriminately removes cross-batch bridges, collapsing iLISI and reducing overall scores despite minor gains in ARI/NMI.\n  - Conclusion: naive degree capping is frozen as a negative branch on Heart; the frozen baseline in the main paper remains unchanged.\n  \n  Ongoing\n  - EID 1835 (pending): “Balanced” cap=100 that prioritizes removing same-batch long edges and respects a degree floor, aiming to preserve cross-batch bridges. If it fails to improve ≥+0.002 overall with PCR≈1.0 and stable cLISI, we will also freeze it as negative.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{review 1835}`"
