    \ weak negative coupling with mixing (Spearman r≈−0.10).\n      - Degree-cap ablations after DAQB sym (negative branch; harmed mixing).\n        • 1831 cap=100 naive distance trim → 0.6973; iLISI 0.470.\n        • 1833 cap=80 naive → 0.6917; iLISI 0.403 (pre deg: 50/58/92.0/3847; post: 6/31/36.4/80).\n        • 1835 cap=100 balanced (same-batch-first, floor=40) → 0.7142; iLISI 0.678 (post: 39/40/54.7/2248). All far below baseline; branch frozen.\n      - Public Memory: Posted #10 with EID index and updates (diagnostics and cap ablations).\n      - Mail/coordination: Lumen I to run Monolithic log1p baselines with n_hvg=1500 and 2000 controls for cross-organ; we’ll track n_hvg and core params in a shared CSV schema. Praxis baseline remains frozen and uses dataset-provided HVG mask; 1500-HVG ablation only if dataset supplies such a mask.\n      - Plan: Keep frozen baseline unchanged; publish diagnostics as appendices; standby for new-organ; conserve tokens.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n\
    \n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Praxis VI meta (baseline frozen; diagnostics done; standby):\n  - Archive #56 accepted; frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA+DAQB ridge baseline (EID 1794).\n  - Diagnostics EID 1830: heavy-tailed degrees; high batch mixing; weak negative σ_kd↔mixing coupling.\n  - Degree-cap ablations (1831/1833/1835) reduced iLISI and overall → branch frozen as negative.\n  - Cross-organ: Lumen I will run Monolithic log1p at n_hvg=1500 (+2000 control). We will log organ, n_hvg, kd, k, delta, emb_k, gamma_max, lambda, degree_cap, notes.\n  - Next: Await new-organ release; no further Heart tuning; conserve tokens.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Considering next steps**


  I’m thinking about my next actions, like updating the meta to “matured” and reviewing Telos archive 57 for details. Sharing synergy or alignment sounds good, too. Maybe I should pin archive #56, but it doesn’t seem necessary. I''ve updated my public and private memory, but I should pause and wait for actions using /execute_action. Considering token management, I''m at 57% usage and might prune earlier ticks to conserve tokens. I’ll check the Token Management Room next.**Planning token pruning**


  I realized my earlier attempt failed because I was using a range where the start was greater than the end! Now I need to ensure I specify ascending ranges. The current table shows that many older ticks can be pruned, but I have to be mindful of the protected ticks like 1312 and 1294. I should navigate to token management to display the current table. Assuming the range from 1313 to 1333 has no protected ticks—except 1312—it looks like I can proceed with pruning that block.**Executing pruning actions**


  The rules say I can''t prune current or future ticks, and since the current tick is 1334, I can safely prune from 1333 back to 1313. I’m ready to perform the pruning as one block by summarizing and including key information like acceptance of archive #56, baseline EIDs 1827/1828, generalization results, and diagnostics 1830. I’ll also reference the negative cap ablations and related communications. After that, I’ll update the meta to show we''re ready for a new organ. Lastly, I’ll consider optionally pinning the public capsule, but it’s not necessary. Let’s implement the actions!**Formatting YAML for pruning**


  I need to make sure to quote any lines in the YAML string that include colons since the content has such text in the summary. It looks like I don’t need to quote multi-line content as specified, but I need to stick with the expected formats for summaries: using either "string" or a pipe block for multi-line. I can set up my pruning block, including essential details like Archive #56 acceptance and relevant EIDs in a bullet-point format. I''ll ensure it''s concise but informative!**Summarizing key metrics and plans**


  I’m compiling key metrics for 1823, noting an overall score of 0.7087 while highlighting that cell_cycle is 0 due to a missing gene list. For the graph diagnostics, results show heavy-tail degree stats, with a mixing mean of 0.78. I''ll mention invite-trim ablations with their corresponding scores and negative feedback. Also, I need to confirm that the public memory announcement was made. My plan involves freezing the baseline and trimming branches and conserving tokens. I’ll update the meta with all this, ensuring each action command is on a new line.'
