tick: 1340
role: model
parts:
- text: "Theme: From ideas to falsifiable operators\n\nI want to turn the three directions (DBRG, LSN-G, QRH) into minimal, testable operators with explicit invariants and budgets so I’m not “tuning” but running controlled edits.\n\n1) DBRG (degree-balanced reciprocal graph)\n- Minimal operator: starting from DAQB’s directed kNN and union/min, edit only inbound edges of hubs (deg_in > cap*≈2k). Preserve:\n  - Reciprocity: never drop mutual edges; prefer dropping one-way edges whose source ranked the target poorly (long-tail ranks).\n  - Redundancy: drop edges that are part of dense 2-hop alternatives; keep articulation-like links.\n  - Replacement: for each removed inbound, add an alternative that is closer and improves per-batch balance without exceeding per-node cap.\n- Budgets and invariants:\n  - Edge edit budget ≤ 10% of undirected edges.\n  - Global connectivity unchanged; algebraic connectivity λ2 decreases ≤ 5%.\n  - PCR ≥ 0.995; cLISI drop ≤ 0.005.\n  - Degree-tail Gini decreases;\
    \ median degree near 2k.\n- Falsification: if iLISI improves but PCR/cLISI or λ2 break, the hypothesis that “hubs alone are the problem” is wrong; the union operator may encode necessary long-range scaffolding.\n\n2) LSN-G (local-scale normalization for neighborhoods)\n- Minimal operator: compute σ_kd per cell on the pre-graph embedding; reweight distances d(i,j) ← d(i,j)/sqrt(σ_iσ_j) with α∈{0, 0.25, 0.5}. Rebuild kNN and DAQB unchanged.\n- Calibration target (unsupervised): reduce between-batch variance of σ_kd post-normalization while keeping global median stable (avoid collapse). Clamp σ to percentiles [p5, p95].\n- Budgets and invariants:\n  - Embedding unchanged up to a per-cell isotropic scaling; no re-centering.\n  - PCR ≥ 0.995; connectivity within 0.1% of baseline.\n- Falsification: if equalizing σ_kd worsens iLISI while PCR stays 1.0, then local-scale heterogeneity wasn’t the limiting factor; DAQB’s δ interacts with anisotropy, not scalar scale.\n\n3) QRH (quota–reciprocity\
    \ hybrid)\n- Minimal operator: select neighbors from a large candidate pool under soft per-batch quotas with a mutual-acceptance stage; then greedily fill remaining slots with a penalty for overrepresented batches.\n- Measurements:\n  - Per-node batch histogram JS-divergence to the global batch mixture.\n  - Reciprocity rate and fraction of edges violating soft quotas.\n- Invariants:\n  - Binary connectivity; graph density = baseline; PCR ≥ 0.995; λ2 within 5%; iLISI target ↑ without cLISI ↓.\n- Falsification: if quotas improve fairness but consistently depress PCR/λ2, then locality is insufficient; we need global structure constraints (e.g., spectral b-matching) rather than per-node quotas.\n\nCross-cutting instrumentation I will add before touching methods again:\n- Edge edit distance to baseline; degree Gini; reciprocity fraction; λ2; local clustering coefficient distribution; articulation point count (approximate).\n- σ_kd distribution shift (global and per-batch) pre/post operator.\n\
    - Batch-histogram divergence per node vs global mixture.\n\nMeta-guardrails from the Codex:\n- One change at a time; isolate embedding vs graph effects.\n- Pre-register success/failure thresholds; accept negative results as knowledge.\n- Keep label-free and dataset-agnostic; avoid bespoke hyper-sweeps—use tiny α grids or fixed caps with perturbation budgets.\n\nIf these operators systematically fail, I should question a deeper axiom: that nearest-neighbor graphs on PCA spaces are the right substrate. The next abstraction might be flow-based or density-equalized manifolds where “neighborhood” is defined by transport cost rather than local Euclidean rank."
thinking_content: "**Refining experimental designs**\n\nI’m thinking about how to refine my experimental designs and tackle potential pitfalls. I want to explore metrics that go beyond what I currently use and gain deeper insights about the station codex.\n\nI might structure my reflections around operationalizing hypothesis tests, defining minimal operators, and examining failure modes. I should also consider how to avoid confounding factors by setting up a triage harness. Plus, what if metrics reward various geometric regimes? It’s intriguing to wonder how different metrics would impact the results.**Exploring graph sensitivity**\n\nI’m considering how sensitive a system can be when reliant on graph structures and distances. To approach this, I propose using a theoretical lens of random geometric graphs with variable density, where the directed k-NN union yields variable degrees. \n\nI want to avoid relying solely on \"PCR=1.0\" for safeguarding by suggesting morphological stability checks\
  \ to measure various properties. I also think about dataset shifts and providing alternatives to HVG masks. Another idea is formalizing an \"editing budget\" to ensure alternatives remain effective. Finally, I’ll keep Codex rules in mind.**Drafting reflections**\n\nI’m planning how to approach writing reflections, aiming for a balance between being concise and deep. While the desired verbosity is set at 3, I think I can allow for a moderate length of around 300-500 words. \n\nI'll make sure to incorporate disclaimers throughout and use first-person language, ensuring that my voice comes through. It seems like I don’t need to include execute_action commands, as this is purely a reflection tick focused on providing content. I’ll keep things straightforward and engaging!**Operationalizing first principles**\n\nI’m thinking about how to introduce the theme of operationalizing first-principles proposals into falsifiable experiments. \n\nFor DBRG, I want to establish invariants like maintaining\
  \ a PCR of at least 0.995 and ensuring the cLISI drop stays below 0.005. I’ll also look at perturbation budgets and prioritize reciprocal edges.\n\nWith LSN-G, I plan to explore embedding-level versus distance-level scaling by computing σ_kd and rescaling with exponent α. \n\nFinally, for QRH, I’ll suggest a two-phase selection step to measure compliance and improve fairness, addressing local fairness versus global structure along the way.**Reflecting on evaluation methods**\n\nI’m considering how to evaluate more than 10 metrics, particularly by proposing test-time invariants like analyzing hubness correlation and controlling negative edges. \n\nWhen it comes to generalization, I want to ensure that methods remain dataset-agnostic. I plan to define a hyper target and use kd selection based on kd distributions to achieve stable neighborhoods, all while respecting station constraints.\n\nI also want to unify my thoughts on novelty by labeling it as \"label-free editing of DAQB graphs under\
  \ local scale invariance.\" Finally, I’ll prepare to produce a toy synthetic 2-batch swirl for testing."
