- text: "`/execute_action{reflect}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Theme: Formalizing the hypotheses into constrained operators\n\n  I. DBRG as a constrained “graph surgery” problem\n  Baseline graph G0=(V,E0) comes from DAQB: directed kNN (k_out=50) → sym-union; undirected degrees are heavy-tailed. Define S(E0) → E’ that edits only non‑mutual inbound edges of hubs to reduce tail while preserving structure.\n\n  Variables and constraints\n  - Keep: all mutual edges M ⊂ E0.\n  - Eligible removals R ⊂ (E0 \\ M) restricted to hubs H={v: deg_in(v)>cap*}, cap*≈2k.\n  - Budget: |ΔE|/|E0| ≤ 0.10 (≤10% edits).\n  - Connectivity: G’ connected; λ2(L_norm(G’)) ≥ (1−ε)·λ2(L_norm(G0)) with ε≤0.05.\n  - Local degree floor: deg(v) ≥ k for all v.\n  - Biology invariants (post hoc, pre-registered): PCR ≥ 0.995; ΔcLISI ≥ −0.005.\n\n  Selection heuristics (label‑free proxies)\n  - Drop priority π_drop(i→j) increases with:\n    • non‑reciprocal; • source‑rank r_i(j) high (long tail); • distance d(i,j) large; • batch‑redundancy\
    \ at j (neighbor batch histogram near-saturated).\n  - Replacement candidates N_alt(i): nearest unused neighbors of i (from cached candidate pool) chosen to (i) minimize d(i,·) and (ii) penalize batch overrepresentation via cost c(i,ℓ)=d(i,ℓ)·(1+β·overrep_i(batch(ℓ))), β small.\n  Predicted effect: degree Gini↓, JS-divergence of per‑node batch histograms to global mix↓, iLISI↑; PCR, cLISI, λ2 stable.\n\n  II. LSN‑G as preconditioning of neighborhood scale\n  Observation: σ_kd varies across batches; a single δ encodes a nonuniform “ruler.” Precondition distances by cellwise scale:\n    d’(i,j)=d(i,j)/((σ_i σ_j)^{α/2}), α∈{0.25,0.5}, with σ_i=kd‑th NN distance in embedding.\n  Guards:\n    - Clamp σ to [p5,p95] to avoid extremes; no re-centering, no axis-specific scaling (isotropic per cell).\n    - Rebuild DAQB unchanged; budgets identical to baseline (same k, kd, δ).\n  Proxies to monitor (unsupervised):\n    - Var_b(σ_kd) across batches↓; global median σ_kd stable (±5%).\n    - Reciprocity\
    \ and λ2 close to baseline.\n  Predictions: modest kBET/iLISI gains if scale heterogeneity was the limiter; failures imply anisotropy (not scalar scale) is primary.\n\n  III. QRH (quota–reciprocity hybrid) as a b‑matching surrogate\n  Start from large candidate matrix C (kq≈1280) on ridge‑residualized PCs:\n    - Stage 1 (mutual core): accept pairs (i,j) if i∈Top_r*(j) and j∈Top_r*(i) and both have per‑batch headroom (soft quotas q_b≈ceil(k/B)).\n    - Stage 2 (fill): greedily add nearest remaining edges with penalty for overrepresented batches within the node’s partial set; stop at k per node.\n  Invariants: binary connectivity; global edge count ≈ baseline; PCR ≥ 0.995; λ2 within 5%.\n  Diagnostics: per‑node batch‑hist JS divergence; reciprocity rate; fraction of quota‑violating edges.\n  Prediction: improved local fairness without fracturing global structure; refutation if λ2/PCR degrade despite small JS gains.\n\n  Complexity and feasibility (n≈20k, k≈50)\n  - Precompute kd and r_i(j)\
    \ from existing neighbor search (O(n·kq)).\n  - DBRG: one pass over hubs; edit budget ≤10% of ~O(n·k) edges; near‑linear.\n  - LSN‑G: O(n·kq) for kd, then same DAQB; memory neutral.\n  - QRH: uses prebuilt candidate lists; greedy b‑matching surrogate O(n·kq log kq) with small constants.\n\n  Pre‑registration (to keep me honest)\n  - Success thresholds: iLISI +0.005 absolute with ΔcLISI ≥ −0.003; PCR ≥ 0.995; λ2 ≥ 0.95× baseline; degree‑Gini −20% (DBRG); Var_b(σ_kd) −20% (LSN‑G).\n  - If thresholds fail twice across seeds, freeze the branch as negative and publish diagnostics explaining the failure modes.\n\n  Codex alignment\n  - These are not hyper‑sweeps; they are targeted, falsifiable edits with conserved budgets and explicit invariants.\n  - Diagnostics (degree Gini, λ2, reciprocity, σ_kd, JS per‑node) make mechanisms legible regardless of headline score.\n```"
