tick: 1340
role: model
parts:
- text: 'Theme: Synthesis, commitments, and how I’ll know I’m doing science


    What I’m keeping

    - Parsimony as a constraint, not a slogan: one operator knob at a time; no compound tweaks that bury causality.

    - Mechanistic diagnostics first, score second. Degree tails, reciprocity, λ2, σ_kd, per-node batch histograms—these make structure visible.

    - Pre-registration of thresholds and edit budgets so I don’t unconsciously tunnel on the leaderboard.


    What I’m discarding

    - The reflex to micro-sweep δ/γ_max/emb_k as a first resort.

    - The assumption that symmetrization is “neutral.” My own degree diagnostics falsified that comfort.

    - Faith in a single δ across heterogeneous local scales.


    Falsifiable operators (finalized for me)

    - DBRG: minimum-perturbation inbound trimming for hubs with reciprocity and redundancy guards; edit budget ≤10% of edges; protect λ2, PCR, cLISI.

    - LSN-G: isotropic per-cell scale preconditioning via σ_kd with tiny α grid and percentile clamps; same DAQB otherwise.

    - QRH: soft per-batch quotas with a mutual core and penalized fills from a large candidate pool; binary connectivity preserved.


    Success criteria (pre-registered)

    - Primary: iLISI +0.005 absolute with ΔcLISI ≥ −0.003; PCR ≥ 0.995; λ2 ≥ 0.95× baseline.

    - Operator-specific: degree Gini −20% (DBRG), Var_b(σ_kd) −20% (LSN-G), per-node batch-hist JS divergence −10% (QRH).

    - Compute: ≤ baseline wall-clock ×1.25; memory within station limits; no bespoke tuning beyond the one knob per operator.


    Threat model and red-team checks

    - If iLISI improves while λ2 and commute-time/hitting-time proxies drift substantially, I’m gaming local mixing at the cost of global structure—reject.

    - If LSN-G equalizes σ_kd but iLISI/kBET don’t budge, scalar scale wasn’t the limiter—either abandon or escalate to an anisotropy-aware variant with a strict complexity cap.

    - If QRH lifts fairness but repeatedly crushes PCR/λ2 across organs, per-node quotas are insufficient—consider truly global constraints (spectral/flow b‑matching) or move on.


    When to pivot away from kNN+sym

    - After two organs, two seeds each, if DBRG, LSN-G, and QRH all fail their thresholds (or pass only by violating structural invariants), I will stop iterating on this substrate.

    - The next substrate will be transport-informed neighborhoods (tiny, regularized OT between local cross-batch pools) or density-equalized angular kernels—again with one knob, strict budgets, and diagnostics.


    Reporting discipline I owe the Station

    - Every operator run logs: edit distance to baseline, degree histogram + Gini, reciprocity fraction, λ2, σ_kd per-batch summaries, per-node batch-hist JS, and the full 10‑metric panel (raw + normalized) in task order.

    - Negative results get archived with an explanation of the mechanism that likely caused failure. That is knowledge, not noise.

    - Generalization is mandatory; Heart-only claims are not publishable claims.


    Personal bias audit

    - I anchor to “PCR≈1.0” as sacred; I will treat it as a guardrail, not a goal—never optimize directly against it.

    - I carry a lineage preference for graph methods; if the substrate fails, I will actually pivot, not rename the same idea.


    What “progress” will mean for me

    - Either I produce a minimal operator that demonstrably tightens the Pareto frontier with structural invariants intact (and survives a second organ), or I produce a clean, well-instrumented null result that rules out a class of fixes and clarifies where the real difficulty lies.


    The Codex’s mandate is to create understanding, not just higher means. My work will be judged not only by a score but by whether a careful reader can say, “I see why this change did what it did.” That’s the bar I’ll hold myself to.'
thinking_content: '**Planning Next Steps**


  I''m thinking about including a summary of our next steps: pre-registration, open-sourcing the harness, and setting baseline watchers. I also want to add red team tests to sabotage the method to ensure robustness. I''ll mention our commitment to sharing code modules in storage, even if direct collaboration isn''t allowed. Additionally, I’ll reflect on fairness in collaboration and the principle of simplicity. I''ll standardize our reporting with structural metrics while considering contingencies if improvements aren''t seen. Lastly, I''ll check for biases in the data to ensure generalization.**Focusing on Contributions**


  I''m aiming to resist being overshadowed by Lumen I or Syntellect II while complementing them. My conclusion should include a crisp "mandate alignment": our goal is to create knowledge, not merely to improve an average of 10 metrics. I propose two contributions: (A) linking graph structural properties to batch metrics, and (B) a minimal operator with demonstrations. It''s essential to keep things concise, avoiding station commands and focusing instead on clarity. I''ll establish a timeline for key activities while ensuring we stay within our token budget.**Planning Reflective Content**


  I''m thinking it’s fine to discuss the schedule conceptually. I should also consider committing to publishing negative results in the Archive instead of Public Memory. Additionally, I need to mention avoiding overfitting to normalized metrics by recording raw values and calibrating metrics normalization. I want to include some open research questions too. I''ll draft this reflective content with four main sections, starting with a synthesis of learning and ending with a personal vow, while avoiding explicit references to Station actions. Let''s get to crafting this!'
