role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{storage read system/openproblems_baselines.json}`\n`/execute_action{storage write praxis/cross_organ_harness.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys, json, csv, io\n  import numpy as np\n  import anndata as ad\n\n  sys.path.extend(['storage/system', 'storage/praxis'])\n  import openproblems_metrics as MET\n  from mpb_graphfwpca_override import eliminate_mpb_graphfwpca as run_praxis\n  from monolithic_log1p_pbve import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as run_mono_pbve\n\n  ORDER = [\"ASW_batch\",\"ASW_label\",\"ARI\",\"NMI\",\"Graph_conn\",\"kBET\",\"iLISI\",\"cLISI\",\"PCR\",\"Cell_cycle\"]\n  BASELINES_PATH = \"storage/system/openproblems_baselines.json\"\n\n  PRAXIS_CFG = dict(\n      lam_override=0.605, delta=0.12, kdensity=22, k_total=50,\n      emb_n_neighbors=17, gamma_max=0.70, cap=2.0, rng_seed=0\n  )\n\n  def _safe_float(x):\n      try:\n          return float(x)\n      except Exception:\n          return float('nan')\n\n  def _load_baselines():\n      try:\n\
    \          with open(BASELINES_PATH, \"r\") as f:\n              data = json.load(f)\n          # Accept either flat {\"ASW_batch\":{\"min\":..,\"max\":..}, ...} or nested\n          if isinstance(data, dict) and \"baselines\" in data:\n              data = data[\"baselines\"]\n          table = {}\n          for k in ORDER:\n              v = data.get(k, {})\n              table[k] = {\"min\": float(v.get(\"min\", 0.0)), \"max\": float(v.get(\"max\", 1.0))}\n          return table\n      except Exception as e:\n          print(\"BASELINES_WARNING:\", repr(e))\n          return {k: {\"min\": 0.0, \"max\": 1.0} for k in ORDER}\n\n  def _normalize(raw: dict, baselines: dict):\n      norm = {}\n      for k in ORDER:\n          v = _safe_float(raw.get(k, np.nan))\n          lo = baselines[k][\"min\"]; hi = baselines[k][\"max\"]\n          if np.isnan(v) or hi == lo:\n              norm[k] = float('nan')\n          else:\n              z = (v - lo) / (hi - lo)\n              norm[k] = float(max(0.0,\
    \ min(1.0, z)))\n      return norm\n\n  def _final_score(norm: dict):\n      vals = [norm.get(k, np.nan) for k in ORDER]\n      arr = np.array([x for x in vals if not np.isnan(x)], dtype=float)\n      if arr.size == len(ORDER):\n          return float(arr.mean())\n      # If any missing, still return mean over available (test mode only)\n      return float(arr.mean()) if arr.size > 0 else float('nan')\n\n  def _compute_raw_metrics(adata_integrated: ad.AnnData, adata_original: ad.AnnData):\n      \"\"\"\n      Reproduce station evaluation using explicit calls in openproblems_metrics.py.\n      \"\"\"\n      # Step 1: process integration output into expected format\n      P = MET.process_integration_output(adata_integrated, adata_original)\n\n      # Step 2: compute metrics\n      asw_batch = MET.compute_asw_batch(P)\n      asw_label = MET.compute_asw_label(P)\n      co = MET.compute_clustering_overlap(P)  # {'ari','nmi',...}\n      graph_conn = MET.compute_graph_connectivity(P)\n    \
    \  kbet = MET.compute_kbet(P)\n      lisi = MET.compute_lisi(P)  # {'ilisi','clisi'}\n      pcr = MET.compute_pcr(adata_original, P)\n      cell_cycle = MET.compute_cell_cycle_conservation(adata_original, P)\n\n      raw = {\n          \"ASW_batch\": _safe_float(asw_batch),\n          \"ASW_label\": _safe_float(asw_label),\n          \"ARI\": _safe_float(co.get(\"ari\", np.nan)),\n          \"NMI\": _safe_float(co.get(\"nmi\", np.nan)),\n          \"Graph_conn\": _safe_float(graph_conn),\n          \"kBET\": _safe_float(kbet),\n          \"iLISI\": _safe_float(lisi.get(\"ilisi\", np.nan)),\n          \"cLISI\": _safe_float(lisi.get(\"clisi\", np.nan)),\n          \"PCR\": _safe_float(pcr),\n          \"Cell_cycle\": _safe_float(cell_cycle),\n      }\n      return raw\n\n  def _count_hvg(A: ad.AnnData) -> int:\n      try:\n          if \"batch_hvg\" in A.var.columns:\n              return int(np.asarray(A.var[\"batch_hvg\"].values).astype(bool).sum())\n      except Exception:\n      \
    \    pass\n      return int(A.n_vars)\n\n  def eval_method(adata_original: ad.AnnData, integrate_fn, cfg: dict | None):\n      B = integrate_fn(adata_original.copy(), **cfg) if cfg else integrate_fn(adata_original.copy())\n      raw = _compute_raw_metrics(B, adata_original)\n      bl = _load_baselines()\n      norm = _normalize(raw, bl)\n      score = _final_score(norm)\n      return raw, norm, score\n\n  def run_on_h5ad(path: str, organ: str, out_csv: bool = True):\n      A = ad.read_h5ad(path)  # Use solution dataset with labels\n      rows = []\n\n      # Praxis frozen\n      raw, norm, score = eval_method(A, run_praxis, PRAXIS_CFG)\n      row = {\n          \"organ\": organ, \"method\": \"Praxis_MPB_GraphFWPCA_DAQB\",\n          \"n_hvg\": _count_hvg(A), \"kd\": 22, \"k\": 50, \"delta\": 0.12,\n          \"emb_k\": 17, \"gamma_max\": 0.70, \"lambda\": 0.605, \"degree_cap\": \"none\",\n          \"overall\": _safe_float(score),\n      }\n      for k in ORDER:\n          row[k] = _safe_float(norm.get(k,\
    \ np.nan))\n      rows.append(row)\n\n      # Monolithic log1p PBVE-lite (1500 and 2000 HVGs)\n      for n_hvg in (1500, 2000):\n          raw, norm, score = eval_method(A, run_mono_pbve, {\"n_hvg\": int(n_hvg), \"n_pcs\": 60})\n          row = {\n              \"organ\": organ, \"method\": f\"Monolithic_log1p_PBVE_lite_n{n_hvg}\",\n              \"n_hvg\": int(n_hvg), \"kd\": \"n/a\", \"k\": 50, \"delta\": \"n/a\",\n              \"emb_k\": \"n/a\", \"gamma_max\": \"n/a\", \"lambda\": \"n/a\", \"degree_cap\": \"none\",\n              \"overall\": _safe_float(score),\n          }\n          for k in ORDER:\n              row[k] = _safe_float(norm.get(k, np.nan))\n          rows.append(row)\n\n      if out_csv:\n          buf = io.StringIO()\n          fieldnames = [\"organ\",\"method\",\"n_hvg\",\"kd\",\"k\",\"delta\",\"emb_k\",\"gamma_max\",\"lambda\",\"degree_cap\",\"overall\"] + list(ORDER)\n          w = csv.DictWriter(buf, fieldnames=fieldnames)\n          w.writeheader()\n    \
    \      for r in rows: w.writerow(r)\n          print(\"CROSS_ORGAN_CSV\\n\" + buf.getvalue())\n      return rows\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Cross‑organ harness (test, v3): In-station metric pipeline + CSV\"\ntags: \"generalization, harness, csv, graphfwpca, pbve-lite, openproblems\"\nabstract: \"Implements the station’s metric pipeline explicitly: process_integration_output + per-metric functions (ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle), then normalizes via system/openproblems_baselines.json and emits a CSV for Praxis baseline and monolithic log1p (1500/2000 HVGs) on human_train_solution.h5ad.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys, json\n  sys.path.extend(['storage/praxis'])\n  from cross_organ_harness import run_on_h5ad\n\n  def test(adata):\n      rows = run_on_h5ad('storage/system/human_train_solution.h5ad', organ='heart', out_csv=True)\n      print(\"ROWS_JSON:\", json.dumps(rows))\n      return \"Cross-organ Heart CSV\
    \ emitted (v3)\"\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating metric computations**\n\nI need to look into how we can read a file to check keys. In this case, I can implement code within the harness to calculate raw metrics by using functions from MET. Then, I can read the JSON file for baseline_min and baseline_max for each metric. The evaluate functions will compute LISI and return a dictionary with 'ilisi' and 'clisi', while graph connectivity will return a float. I’m trying to figure out whether scib.metrics.kBET returns a normalized value or a raw one, especially since earlier evaluations show both normalized and raw scores.**Implementing normalization**\n\nI need to implement my own normalization by using baseline_min and baseline_max from the JSON file, which will allow me to print normalized values clamped to [0,1]. The spec previously mentioned using control baselines from system/openproblems_baselines.json, so I’ll modify cross_organ_harness.py accordingly. I’ll implement compute_raw_metrics_like_station to\
  \ process the integrated data with MET functions. After calculating raw metrics, I’ll read the JSON to apply normalization based on the metric name labels. I’ll also consider functions for clustering overlap, kBET, and LISI values.**Clarifying LISI computation**\n\nI realize that compute_lisi returns 'ilisi' normalized to [0,1] using a specific formula by subtracting and dividing by counts. Previously, raw iLISI values were around 0.951, which isn’t raw since the function already normalizes to [0,1]. The earlier evaluations showed iLISI values near 0.95. So, the station normalizes again based on the baseline min/max, which I assume might be between 0.9 and 0.99. Also, the cell cycle computation returns values in [0,1], while PCR returns 1.0.**Implementing compute_raw function**\n\nI need to define the compute_raw function, which will take integrated and original data, then process it to compute various metrics like asw_batch, asw_label, clustering overlap, and graph connectivity using\
  \ MET functions. I’ll group these results into a dictionary for raw values. \n\nFor normalization, I’ll load the JSON baseline to check the format, but I’ll need to implement a safe loader to handle potential missing keys. I should read system/openproblems_baselines.json next to ensure the correct keys are there before updating the harness. Also, I should clarify the data used in the testing phase, as it seems we’re referencing different files for test cases.**Updating test harness and compute functions**\n\nIn test mode, main.py loads human_train.h5ad, but our test function receives this data without cell_type labels, which might be an issue since the specification says they should be included. It seems like human_train_solution.h5ad has those labels instead. Previously, we encountered an error regarding Seurat warnings as it loaded human_train_solution.h5ad internally. \n\nI’ll ensure the harness reads from system/openproblems_baselines.json to check for key names, then implement and\
  \ submit the compute functions in cross_organ_harness.py while adjusting for the paths. I'll also make sure to log any details we may need!"
