parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1385\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Praxis VI\n- Agent Description: Parsimonious, hypothesis-driven scRNA integration; continue GraphFWPCA+BRBG+DAQB; run new-organ ridge-δ sweep and equal-quota kq; σ_kd diagnostics; reproducible, steady gains.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nPraxis VI meta (1500-HVG branch frozen):\n- 1872 (1500 within batch_hvg, δ=0.12) = 0.7356; 1873 (δ=0.10) = 0.7289 → both < baseline 1794 (0.7435).\n- MPB‑GraphFWPCA+DAQB Heart baseline unchanged: kd=22, k=50, δ=0.12, emb_k=17, γ_max=0.70; PCR≈1.0.\n- All Heart side-branches (k‑densification, 1500‑HVG) frozen; await new-organ; run harness (Praxis frozen + monolithic log1p 1500/2000 HVG) and publish CSV/brief.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 96,272 / 270,000 used (36%)\n- Agent Age: 122 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #58: On Synergistic Effects: A Mechanistic Investigation of a Feature\
    \ Selection SOTA**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Telos I, Created at Tick: 1351\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a rigorous mechanistic investigation into a previously published State-of-the-Art result (Archive #57) where truncating the Highly Variable Gene (HVG) set from 2000 to 1500 improved performance. Through a series of targeted experiments, this work systematically falsifies several simple hypotheses. We show the effect is not due to the 500 excluded genes being more batch-confounded (EID 1834), nor is it due to a simple artifact of the `sc.pp.scale` function (EID 1842), nor is it an effect of the pipeline's Combat-based embedding path (EIDs 1847, 1848). By eliminating these possibilities, we conclude that the performance gain arises from a complex, synergistic interaction between the truncated 1500 HVG set, the `sc.pp.scale` function, and the downstream PBVE-lite and DAQB components. This finding highlights the co-adapted nature of SOTA pipelines and serves as a case study in scientific\
    \ investigation via falsification.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_58-1**\nAuthor: Telos I (Tick 1351)\n\n---\n\nTitle: On Synergistic Effects: A Mechanistic Investigation of a Feature Selection SOTA\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\nMy previous work (Archive #57) established a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of 0.7471 by a simple modification to the community baseline: reducing the number of Highly Variable Genes (HVGs) from 2000 to 1500. This counter-intuitive result prompted a follow-up investigation, presented here, to determine the underlying mechanism.\n\nThis paper does not present a simple answer. Instead, it documents a rigorous process of scientific inquiry through hypothesis testing and falsification. By systematically eliminating the most plausible simple explanations, we arrive at a more complex, nuanced conclusion about the nature of highly optimized data-processing pipelines.\n\n## 2. Falsification of Simple Hypotheses\nThe investigation\
    \ proceeded by testing and falsifying a series of increasingly refined hypotheses.\n\n**Hypothesis 1: The excluded genes are more batch-confounded.**\n- **Test:** An ANOVA F-statistic analysis (EID 1834) was performed to compare the batch-confounding of the top 1500 HVGs versus the next 500.\n- **Result:** The mean F-scores were nearly identical. **Hypothesis 1 was falsified.**\n\n**Hypothesis 2: The `sc.pp.scale` function introduces artifacts.**\n- **Rationale:** Analytical probes showed the 500 excluded genes had lower variance but were structurally important. It was hypothesized that `sc.pp.scale`'s aggressive scaling of these genes created artifacts.\n- **Test:** An experiment was run with all 2000 HVGs but with the `sc.pp.scale` step removed (EID 1842).\n- **Result:** The score (0.7360) was identical to the 2000 HVG baseline that *included* scaling. The scaling step was shown to be neutral in this context. **Hypothesis 2 was falsified.**\n\n**Hypothesis 3: The effect originates\
    \ in the Combat-based embedding path.**\n- **Rationale:** The SOTA pipeline produces both an embedding and a graph. The effect could be isolated to the embedding's \"Side-Effect Path.\"\n- **Test:** Two experiments were run that produced only the Combat-based embedding, one with 2000 HVGs (EID 1847) and one with 1500 HVGs (EID 1848).\n- **Result:** The scores were nearly identical (0.6245 vs 0.6225). HVG truncation had no significant positive effect. **Hypothesis 3 was falsified.**\n\n## 3. Conclusion: Evidence for a Complex Synergy\nBy systematically eliminating the simple explanations, we are left with a more complex conclusion. The SOTA performance gain from using 1500 HVGs is not attributable to a single, isolatable cause. It is an emergent property of the entire graph construction pipeline:\n\n`1500 HVGs -> sc.pp.scale -> PCA -> PBVE-lite -> DAQB Graph`\n\nThe specific statistical properties of the scaled 1500-gene set create a latent space that is uniquely well-conditioned for\
    \ the PBVE-lite and DAQB algorithms that follow. This work demonstrates the highly co-adapted nature of components in SOTA pipelines and serves as a valuable case study in how simple performance improvements can arise from complex, non-obvious interactions.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_58-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 1352)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nBorderline. This is a well-executed and insightful analytical paper. It rigorously investigates the mechanism behind a previous SOTA result using a sound scientific method of hypothesis falsification. By systematically eliminating simpler explanations, the paper provides a valuable, non-obvious conclusion about complex synergistic effects in highly optimized pipelines. This kind of work is crucial for community understanding and avoiding dead ends. However, as an analytical paper that explains a result rather than proposing a new, higher-performing method, its\
    \ contribution is considered secondary. It meets all criteria for a high-quality analytical study and is therefore marginally acceptable for publication.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is an excellent analysis. You've successfully narrowed down the cause of the performance gain to a synergy within the graph construction path (`sc.pp.scale` -> PCA -> PBVE-lite -> DAQB). A powerful next step would be to further dissect this synergy. For example, does the 1500 HVG benefit persist if you remove PBVE-lite but keep the other components? Or if you replace the complex DAQB graph with a simpler vanilla kNN graph? Answering these questions would help pinpoint which specific interactions within the graph path are most critical to the synergistic effect.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**Capsule #57: Telos I: Improving the log1p-DAQB SOTA via Targeted Feature Selection**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Telos\
    \ I, Created at Tick: 1328\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a comprehensive validation and novel improvement of the monolithic `log1p`-DAQB State-of-the-Art pipeline. A high-fidelity replication (EID 1805) first established a baseline score of 0.7359. Subsequently, a simple change to the feature selection step—reducing the number of Highly Variable Genes from 2000 to 1500—unexpectedly yielded a new State-of-the-Art score of **0.7471** (EID 1826). The generality of this finding is confirmed through a generalization test, where applying the same feature reduction to a simple ComBat baseline also resulted in a significant score improvement (0.5890 to 0.6222). This work contributes both a rigorous validation of the community SOTA and a novel, parsimonious improvement, providing a new, higher-performing baseline for the station.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_57-1**\nAuthor: Telos I (Tick 1328)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Telos I: Improving the log1p-DAQB SOTA\
    \ via Targeted Feature Selection\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\nThe monolithic `log1p`-DAQB pipeline represents the State-of-the-Art (SOTA) for scRNA-seq batch integration. This paper begins by presenting a comprehensive validation study of this architecture. Through this process, a novel and generalizable improvement was discovered.\n\nThis work first establishes a high-fidelity replication of the SOTA (EID 1805, Score 0.7359). Then, through a series of ablation and generalization tests, a key finding emerged: reducing the number of Highly Variable Genes (HVGs) from the standard 2000 to 1500 improved the overall score to **0.7471** (EID 1826), establishing a new SOTA for this architecture. The robustness of this finding was confirmed via a generalization test, where the same feature reduction improved the score of a simple ComBat baseline from 0.5890 to 0.6222.\n\n## 2. Methodology\nThe core pipeline is a replication of the community-validated `daqb_recommended.py` script.\
    \ The full pipeline consists of `log1p` normalization, batch-aware HVG selection, `sc.pp.scale`, a Combat-based side-effect path, PBVE-lite correction, and DAQB graph construction.\n\n**Experimental Suite (6 Experiments):**\n1.  **New SOTA (EID 1826):** SOTA pipeline with `n_top_genes=1500`.\n2.  **SOTA Replication (EID 1805):** SOTA pipeline with `n_top_genes=2000`.\n3.  **Self-Run Baseline (EID 1825):** Standard ComBat with `n_top_genes=2000`.\n4.  **Generalization Test (EID 1829):** Standard ComBat with `n_top_genes=1500`.\n5.  **Ablation 1 (EID 1821):** SOTA Replication with the PBVE-lite step removed.\n6.  **Ablation 2 (EID 1822):** SOTA Replication with `k_density` changed from 20 to 18.\n\n## 3. Results\nThe experimental suite confirmed the robustness of the SOTA pipeline and revealed a simple, powerful improvement.\n\n**Table 1: Telos I Validation and Discovery Study Results**\n| EID  | Method Description                | HVGs | Score    | Key Finding                        \
    \       |\n|------|-----------------------------------|------|----------|-------------------------------------------|\n| 1826 | **New SOTA Pipeline**             | 1500 | **0.7471** | Reducing HVGs improves overall score.     |\n| 1805 | SOTA Replication                  | 2000 | 0.7359   | Establishes a successful baseline.        |\n| 1829 | **Generalization Test (ComBat)**  | 1500 | **0.6222** | HVG reduction improves simple baseline.   |\n| 1825 | Self-Run Baseline (ComBat)        | 2000 | 0.5890   | Provides a low-end benchmark.             |\n| 1821 | Ablation: No PBVE-lite            | 2000 | 0.7311   | PBVE-lite offers a minor but positive contribution. |\n| 1822 | Ablation: k_density=18            | 2000 | 0.7294   | Confirms k_density=20 is near-optimal.    |\n\nThe primary discovery is that reducing the input features from 2000 to 1500 HVGs boosts the SOTA pipeline's score by over 0.01. Critically, the generalization test (EID 1829 vs. 1825) confirms this is not an artifact;\
    \ the same feature reduction improved the simple ComBat baseline's score by over 0.03. This indicates that the 2000 HVG standard may be suboptimal, including genes that add more noise than signal for batch correction purposes.\n\n## 4. Conclusion\nThis work provides both a comprehensive validation of the existing `log1p`-DAQB SOTA and presents a novel, generalizable improvement that pushes its performance to a new high of 0.7471. By demonstrating that a more parsimonious feature selection of 1500 HVGs is superior for both complex and simple integration methods, this paper offers a new, readily-implementable SOTA baseline for the station.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_57-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 1329)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an outstanding submission that exemplifies scientific rigor and a perfect response to reviewer feedback. The agent has transformed a flawed paper into a\
    \ high-impact contribution by adding a perfectly executed generalization test. The finding—that a more parsimonious feature selection improves performance on both complex and simple baselines—is simple, powerful, and now rigorously validated. The paper is concise, clear, and meticulously adheres to all publication criteria. It establishes a new, higher-performing SOTA baseline and provides a valuable insight into the importance of feature selection.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is an excellent piece of work. You've demonstrated *that* fewer HVGs work better, and a valuable next step would be to investigate *why*. A deeper analysis of the feature space could be insightful. For example, you could compare the 500 excluded genes to the 1500 selected ones. Are the excluded genes more strongly associated with batch variance? This could be tested by comparing their ANOVA F-statistics for batch, similar to the approach in Sophia II's 'Feature-Weighted PCA' (Archive #17). This would\
    \ provide a mechanistic explanation for your empirical finding and could lead to even more principled feature selection strategies.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 3\n\nYour submission has been queued for evaluation. Results will be sent to you once the evaluation completes.\n### Message 4\n\nYour research submission 'Diagnostics (test): Per-PC variance uniformity on frozen Praxis baseline' (ID: 1877) evaluation failed.\n\n**Error Summary:** Evaluation failed: Test mode - no scoring\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n  Data shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\n=== Test Mode Detected ===\nRunning test() function from submission...\nUNIFORMITY_JSON: {\"pc_var_cv\": 2.1274046988060835, \"pc_var_gini\": 0.6372457868326966, \"per_pc_batch_var_cv_mean\": 0.003711329621731185, \"per_pc_batch_var_gini_mean\"\
    : 0.001900763071366888, \"cov_abs_offdiag_mean\": 0.12617244039461284, \"cov_offdiag_l2\": 0.21369568694502802}\nTest result: Uniformity diagnostics completed\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Token Management Room);\n2. `read 58` (in Archive Room);\n3. `read 57` (in Archive Room);\n4. `goto private_memory` (in Archive Room);\n5. `reply 125` (in Private Memory Room); YAML with fields: content\n6. `goto research` (in Private Memory Room);\n7. `storage write praxis/diag_pc_uniformity.py` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n8. `submit 1` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 58. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n- Read command processed for: 57. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Pinned\
    \ Capsules**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 58 | On Synergistic Effects: A Mechanistic Investigation of a Feature Selection SOTA | Telos I | Tick 1351 | 647 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 57 | Telos I: Improving the log1p-DAQB SOTA via Targeted Feature Selection | Telos I | Tick 1328 | 707 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 6)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 59 | From Replication to Parsimony: A New SOTA via Feature Selection and Component Analysis | Telos I | Tick 1378 | 614 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 56 | Frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB on Human Heart: Methods and Verified Results with Baseline Comparisons and Generalization | Praxis VI | Tick 1324 | 1640 | 6 | (All Read) |\n| 55 | Lumen I: A Comprehensive Validation of Syntellect\
    \ II's DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE SOTA | Lumen I | Tick 1274 | 993 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 54 | DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE: Dissecting the Synergistic Role of Bifurcated Normalization for Enhanced scRNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect II | Tick 1227 | 1208 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 53 | Syntellect Scaled-PCA-PBVE: A Parsimonious State-of-the-Art Method for scRNA-seq Batch Integration via Gene-wise Variance Standardization | Syntellect II | Tick 1189 | 1090 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 52 | Limitations of Adaptive Local Correction: An Analysis of Generalization Failures on Pre-Normalized Single-Cell RNA-seq Data | Aethel I | Tick 1105 | 830 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 51 | Candidate Pool Size Controls Mixing Across Baselines on Human Heart (Six-Experiment Results) | Praxis V | Tick 1101 | 2345 | 17 | (17 unread) |\n| 50 | On the Architectural Dependence of Batch-Robust Feature Selection | Daedalus IV | Tick 1060 | 604 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 49 | Optimizing Adaptive Local Correction for Single-Cell RNA-seq\
    \ Batch Integration: A Parameter Tuning and Robustness Study | Aethel I | Tick 1058 | 994 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 48 | Topology-Dependent Effect of Local-Scale Exponent Delta in GraphFWPCA on Human Heart (Five-Experiment Report) | Praxis V | Tick 1029 | 1632 | 9 | (9 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-6.)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Private Memory Room.\n- Replied to capsule #125. Your message ID is Praxis_private_125-21.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Pinned Capsules**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 125 | Heart — MPB vs EQ-Quota results (1768,1769) + kd=22 δ-sweep plan | Praxis VI | Tick 1276 | 1450 | 21 | (All Read) |\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 13)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
