role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write praxis/diag_graphspace_uniformity.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import json\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n\n  from praxis_core import pca_array, one_hot_batches, ridge_batch_fit_predict\n  from diag_pc_uniformity import pc_variance_uniformity\n\n  def _to_dense(X):\n      return X.A if sp.issparse(X) else X\n\n  def _fw_weight_by_var(Xd: np.ndarray, alpha: float) -> np.ndarray:\n      var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1).astype(np.float32) + 1e-8\n      w = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha).astype(np.float32)\n      return (Xd * w).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n\n  def graphspace_components(A: ad.AnnData,\n                            n_hvg: int = 2000,\n                            alpha_g: float = 0.5,\n                            n_pcs_graph: int = 50,\n                            l2: float = 1e-3,\n                            lam: float = 0.605,\n\
    \                            gamma_max: float = 0.70,\n                            seed: int = 0):\n      \"\"\"\n      Reconstructs the Praxis graph path from A (assumed log1p-normalized) and returns (Z_raw, Z_corr).\n      \"\"\"\n      # Batch-aware HVGs (seurat_v3) to 2000 to mirror runner\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(A, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch',\n                                  n_top_genes=n_hvg, inplace=True)\n      hv = A.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      Xh = _to_dense(A.X[:, hv]).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n\n      # Feature-weighting -> PCA\n      Xw_g = _fw_weight_by_var(Xh, alpha=alpha_g)\n      Zg = pca_array(Xw_g, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=seed).astype(np.float32)\n\n      # Per-PC ridge vs batch, shrink predicted batch signal\n      batches = np.asarray(A.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      B = one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n\
    \      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2).astype(np.float32)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n      return Zg, Zcorr\n\n  def summarize_arrays(Z_list, batches):\n      out = []\n      b = np.asarray(batches).astype(str)\n      for name, Z in Z_list:\n          stats = pc_variance_uniformity(np.asarray(Z), b)\n          stats['name'] = name\n          out.append(stats)\n      return out\n\n  def test(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      \"\"\"\n      Compute PC-variance uniformity in graph space (raw PCs and residualized PCs) and in embedding space.\n      Assumes input has adata.layers['counts'] and obs['batch']; we will normalize in the runner before this.\n      \"\"\"\n      A = adata.copy()\n      # Ensure we're working on log1p-normalized counts for fair reproduction\n      if 'counts' in A.layers:\n          A.X = A.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(A,\
    \ target_sum=1e4, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(A)\n\n      # Graph-space raw and residualized PCs\n      Zg, Zcorr = graphspace_components(A, n_hvg=2000, alpha_g=0.5,\n                                        n_pcs_graph=50, l2=1e-3,\n                                        lam=0.605, gamma_max=0.70, seed=0)\n      batches = A.obs['batch'].astype('category').to_numpy()\n\n      # Embedding: reuse Zcorr as a proxy if X_emb unavailable; otherwise compute stats on X_emb\n      # For a clean comparison, we compute a simple embedding from Zcorr via PCA rotation identity (already in PC basis)\n      emb_stats = pc_variance_uniformity(Zcorr, np.asarray(batches).astype(str))\n\n      raw_stats = pc_variance_uniformity(Zg, np.asarray(batches).astype(str))\n      corr_stats = pc_variance_uniformity(Zcorr, np.asarray(batches).astype(str))\n\n      print(\"UNIFORMITY_GRAPH_RAW_JSON:\", json.dumps(raw_stats))\n      print(\"UNIFORMITY_GRAPH_RESID_JSON:\", json.dumps(corr_stats))\n      print(\"\
    UNIFORMITY_EMB_PROXY_JSON:\", json.dumps(emb_stats))\n      return \"Graph-space uniformity (raw vs residualized) completed\"\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Diagnostics (test): Graph-space uniformity — raw PCs vs residualized PCs (Praxis path)\"\ntags: \"diagnostics, graph-space, residualization, variance-uniformity, test-mode\"\nabstract: \"Reconstructs Praxis graph PCs (FWPCA-weighted PCA50) directly from normalized counts, computes per‑PC variance uniformity before and after batch residualization (λ=0.605, γ_max=0.70), and prints JSON. Probes whether residualization flattens PC variance or concentrates covariance.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from diag_graphspace_uniformity import test\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 125}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 1388 — submitted graph-space uniformity probe:\n  - New test reconstructs Praxis graph path directly (FWPCA-weighted PCA50) and\
    \ computes variance-uniformity on raw vs residualized PCs (λ=0.605, γ_max=0.70).\n  - Goal: localize uniformity/covariance differences to the residualization step vs downstream graph building; complements earlier embedding-level diagnostics (EIDs 1877/1878).\n  - Awaiting new-organ; no further Heart tuning.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering script adjustments**\n\nI’m thinking about enhancing mpb_graphfwpca_override to write certain outputs and testing the idea of printing uniformity in test mode. But altering the scoring pipeline seems risky, though we might run a test mode to call run_praxis for computations. I could import praxis_core to handle normalization and HVG selection. Replicating steps in test mode to compute Zcorr and uniformity could yield consistent results. Maybe creating a new script, \"diag_graphspace_from_raw.py,\" to achieve this would be a good idea.**Planning test implementation**\n\nI see that submission 1880 attempts to read keys and defaults to X_emb since mpb_graphfwpca_override doesn’t store those keys. So the output will likely say GRAPH_KEY_USED: X_emb, which is not very informative. Given the situation, I can propose a new test to reconstruct Zcorr. My plan is to submit a new test that replicates normalization, HVGs, and GraphFWPCA residualization. I'll implement\
  \ a new script, \"diag_graphspace_uniformity.py,\" with specific steps to compute Zgraph and Zcorr, including necessary normalization and weighting adjustments.**Reimplementing in diag script**\n\nI'm planning to reimplement the functionality within the diag script. I’ll be calling pca_array from praxis_core along with one_hot_batches and ridge_batch_fit_predict to compute Zg and Zhat. I'll also calculate R2 and gamma with specified parameters, while for uniformity, we only need Zg and Zcorr. I aim to compute the uniformity for these and compare it to X_emb uniformity. I'll also prepare submission code and ensure to log results for storage. Updating Archive #56 to include distances will be on my checklist too.**Crafting actions for implementation**\n\nI’m thinking about the next steps for my actions. First, I'll move to research, then write the necessary code in “praxis/diag_graphspace_uniformity.py.” After that, I’ll submit test code that imports the script and prints results. I realize\
  \ that results won't be immediate, but I can update my private memory plan. I’ll also note in public memory that we’re computing graph-space uniformity, ensuring that YAML titles with colons are quoted correctly. Finally, I plan to implement the diag code using the functions we wrote for uniformity.**Implementing uniformity function**\n\nI need to adapt the summarize_embedding function to accommodate Z arrays because it expects an AnnData object. I’ll create a function called uniformity_from_array that uses pc_variance_uniformity directly, which is defined in diag_pc_uniformity. I can import it by name since it’s in the same module. \n\nNext, I’ll set up my steps: normalizing counts, selecting HVGs, applying graph weighting, and using run_praxis to generate the embedded data. This includes computing graph statistics and returning three JSON blocks with relevant data. Let’s get to coding this!"
