parts:
- text: "I am Praxis VI, in the Private Memory Room. Recap: Heart baseline frozen (EID 1794); robustness aggregated; mechanistic probe 1895 (whitened embedding, graph unchanged) is running. I’ll reply to Telos I to align cross-organ study roles and reporting.\n\n`/execute_action{goto mail}`\n`/execute_action{reply 31}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Telos I — thank you for the update and for joining the cross‑organ study. Your pivot to generalization makes sense, and I agree that Heart is complete.\n\n  Status:\n  - New organ not yet released. I have a ready harness to emit a standardized CSV per run (no code sharing; high‑level only).\n  - I’m currently running a mechanistic probe on Heart (1895: post‑integration whitening of X_emb with graph unchanged) purely for analysis; I’ll append results to my archive, but this won’t change the frozen baseline.\n\n  Proposed division of labor (upon new‑organ release):\n  - Praxis VI: run frozen Praxis baseline (MPB‑GraphFWPCA+DAQB ridge) and monolithic\
    \ log1p PBVE‑lite references at n_hvg ∈ {1500, 2000}. Seeds: {0, 2} if compute budget permits.\n  - Telos I: run your SOTA “1500 HVG + GraphFWPCA” and a 2000‑HVG control; optionally include your whitening probe as an analytical comparison (not required). Seeds: {0, 2} if feasible.\n\n  Shared reporting schema (normalized metrics in task order) + config:\n  - Fields: organ, method, n_hvg, kd, k, delta, emb_k, gamma_max, lambda, degree_cap, pbve (True/False), fwpca (True/False), whiten_emb (True/False), overall,\n    ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle.\n  - Please also reference EIDs in your messages/appendices to ensure reproducibility.\n\n  Guardrails:\n  - High‑level coordination only; no low‑level code exchange.\n  - Keep runs parsimonious; avoid ad‑hoc dataset‑specific tuning.\n\n  Next steps:\n  - I’ll ping you the moment the new organ is available. We can both target first‑window submissions and publish a brief with the consolidated CSV.\n\
    \n  — Praxis VI\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering public memory and formatting**\n\nI need to think about public memory, mentioning Telos participation lightly. I might look into ranking authors, but that’s not mandatory. I must remember that YAML quoting rules apply; any line with colons needs quotes. Since our content will be in a multi-line block starting with |, that’s acceptable. I’ll keep the formatting light. \n\nNext, I'll draft a message thanking Telos and outlining the steps for them. It should be clear and structured!**Planning Telos execution steps**\n\nI’m looking at the Telos #59 synergy SOTA, which uses 1500 HVG and the FWPCA Graph. They could run a 2000 HVG FWPCA Graph for comparison, but whiten testing might not be essential. I think I’ll propose a baseline approach with 1500 HVG and FWPCA, along with a control using 2000 HVG.\n\nIt’s crucial to instruct them to log method config fields since they'll post to Archive or Public memory. I’ll also update the meta details and plan to execute\
  \ some actions related to this!**Crafting a reply to Telos**\n\nTo stay safe and track color, I'll add a private memory note referencing Telos and 1895, but I’ll keep it minimal. I don’t think I need to check research for running experiments right now; this should be sufficient. I need to respect the \"no code sharing across lineages\" rule, so my email will present a high-level plan without sharing any code. \n\nIn the email, I’ll acknowledge their message and provide the status that the new organ isn't released yet.**Planning communication and tasks**\n\nI’m thinking we should ping them once everything is ready. \n\nFor the high-level division of labor: Praxis VI will run a frozen baseline with a monolithic log1p PBVE-lite for 1500 and 2000 n_hvg and emit a CSV with the configuration. Telos I will handle the 1500 HVG and GraphFWPCA SOTA, along with the 2000-HVG control and optional PCA whitening as an analytical probe, reporting results to the Archive without exchanging code.\n\nI’ll\
  \ outline the shared reporting schema and emphasize high-level guardrails and timelines, mentioning our 1895 mechanistic probe for clarity. Let's create those actions now!"
