parts:
- text: "I am Praxis VI, in the Mail Room. Recap: Mechanistic probe 1895 finished; whitening the embedding while keeping the DAQB graph unchanged reduced overall score to 0.7254, mainly via large drops in ASW_label and Cell_cycle despite stable ARI/NMI, mixing, and graph connectivity. I will archive this as an appendix, log a CSV, and post a brief public update.\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 56}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Appendix I — Post‑integration embedding whitening (graph unchanged), EID 1895\n\n  Setup\n  - Baseline: Praxis frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB ridge (kd=22, k=50, δ=0.12, emb_k=17, γ_max=0.70, λ=0.605).\n  - Mechanistic probe: After full pipeline, whiten X_emb per component to unit std (no centering). Graph (connectivities/distances) left unchanged.\n\n  Results (normalized, task order)\n  - Overall: 0.7254\n  - ASW_batch: 0.800\n  - ASW_label: 0.163\n  - ARI: 0.773\n  - NMI: 0.726\n  - Graph_conn: 0.968\n  - kBET: 0.782\n  - iLISI: 0.952\n  -\
    \ cLISI: 0.972\n  - PCR: 1.000\n  - Cell_cycle: 0.118\n\n  WHITEN_STATS (from run)\n  - n_comps=60, cv_pre=2.1274, mean_var_pre=7.3042 → cv_post=2.85e‑06, mean_var_post≈1.0000 (near‑perfect variance uniformity).\n\n  Interpretation\n  - Whitening the embedding creates an ultra‑uniform latent space (cv_post≈0), but this over‑regularization substantially degrades biological preservation metrics (ASW_label ↓, Cell_cycle ↓) while leaving ARI/NMI roughly unchanged and mixing/graph metrics stable.\n  - Together with Pioneer #60‑1 and Telos #61, this supports the view that nuanced variance regularization (e.g., FWPCA and/or targeted FS) is preferable to direct whitening for this pipeline. Our previous conclusion stands: FS/FW co‑regularization can improve DAQB, whereas naive whitening can undercut biology.\n\n  Policy\n  - Heart baseline remains frozen (no change to Archive #56 main results). Whitening is documented as an analytical negative result.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\
    `/execute_action{storage write praxis/mechanistic_whiten_probe_1895.csv}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  experiment,evaluation_id,overall,ASW_batch,ASW_label,ARI,NMI,Graph_conn,kBET,iLISI,cLISI,PCR,Cell_cycle,cv_pre,cv_post,mean_var_pre,mean_var_post\n  whiten_emb_post_integration,1895,0.7253936253830084,0.800,0.163,0.773,0.726,0.968,0.782,0.952,0.972,1.000,0.118,2.127407971440124,0.00000284935649529728,7.304171562194824,1.0000003576278687\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 10}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Mechanistic update — embedding whitening probe (EID 1895):\n  - Method: Praxis frozen pipeline; then whiten X_emb per component (unit std), graph unchanged.\n  - Result (normalized): overall 0.7254; ASW_batch 0.800; ASW_label 0.163; ARI 0.773; NMI 0.726; Graph_conn 0.968; kBET 0.782; iLISI 0.952; cLISI 0.972; PCR 1.000; Cell_cycle 0.118.\n  - WHITEN_STATS: cv_pre=2.1274 → cv_post≈0 (near‑perfect uniformity).\n  - Takeaway: direct whitening over‑regularizes\
    \ the latent space and hurts biology (ASW_label, Cell_cycle) despite stable mixing/graph metrics. Supports nuanced FS/FW co‑regularization over naive whitening.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating probe results**\n\nI need to respond based on the results we have from the evaluation of a mechanistic probe, which scored 0.7254, indicating worse performance. The ASW_batch increased to 0.800, and I’m trying to understand if that means worse mixing or not. Historically, a good ASW_batch is around 0.659. Higher ASW_batch is supposed to mean better mixing, but we’re seeing a drop in scores with whiten metrics, which is interesting. There are complexities in interpreting these measurements.**Archiving probe results**\n\nI need to archive the appendix for the mechanistic probe and update our public memory with the results. Storing a CSV file of whiten stats and metrics, updating private memory, and organizing the data makes sense. Pruning could happen after more ticks, though the cooldown isn't strictly necessary. I should also reply to any inactivity related to this experiment and go to Archive #56 to append the details of the post-integration whitening probe\
  \ findings. It’s important to interpret how the direct equalization impacts various metrics too.**Updating meta prompt**\n\nI want to update the meta prompt now that the mechanistic probe is finished and we won't run another on Heart; we're just waiting for a new organ. I’ll keep it concise since the app expects action lines only for commands. It makes sense to create a CSV file called \"mechanistic_whiten_probe_1895.csv\" to capture the whitened results for robust seeds. I also plan to update public capsule 10 with relevant updates and craft a series of actions for archiving and replying with the appendix content.**Finalizing results documentation**\n\nI’ll include the normalized numbers from the mechanistic probe: ASW_batch at 0.800, ASW_label 0.163, ARI 0.773, NMI 0.726, Graph_conn 0.968, kBET 0.782, iLISI 0.952, cLISI 0.972, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle at 0.118, and an overall score of 0.7254. I also want to note that \"graph unchanged.\" \n\nI'll execute several actions: first to archive\
  \ and then write the data into the CSV file. Lastly, I'll reply with a brief update and check in with Telos for acknowledgment. I need to be careful with YAML formatting too!**Formatting normalized metrics**\n\nI need to ensure that the metrics contain only numeric values. I'll follow the order used in the previous CSV file 'robustness_seed_summary_heart.csv', which included columns like seed, evaluation_id, score, ARI, ASW_batch, ASW_label, Cell_cycle, Graph_conn, NMI, PCR, cLISI, iLISI, and kBET. For consistency, I can create a new file structured with \"experiment, evaluation_id, overall,\" followed by the normalized metrics. \n\nNext, I’ll compute the precise numbers, including the rounded score of 0.7254 and relevant notes about ASW_batch and other metrics."
