tick: 1432
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Reciprocity and Hubness diagnostics (test): Frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA+DAQB baseline\"\ntags: \"diagnostics, reciprocity, hubness, graph-health\"\nabstract: \"Runs the frozen Praxis baseline, then quantifies kNN reciprocity (mutual edges) and hubness (degree tail, Gini) and their coupling with batch mixing. Emits JSON blocks; no scoring.\"\ncontent: |\n  import json, numpy as np\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scipy.sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  import sys\n  sys.path.extend(['storage/praxis'])\n  from mpb_graphfwpca_override import eliminate_mpb_graphfwpca as run_frozen\n\n  CFG = dict(lam_override=0.605, delta=0.12, kdensity=22, k_total=50,\n             emb_n_neighbors=17, gamma_max=0.70, cap=2.0, rng_seed=0)\n\n  def _gini(x: np.ndarray) -> float:\n      x = np.asarray(x, dtype=float).ravel()\n      x = x[x >= 0]\n      if x.size == 0: return 0.0\n      x_sorted\
    \ = np.sort(x)\n      n = x_sorted.size\n      cumx = np.cumsum(x_sorted, dtype=float)\n      return float((n + 1 - 2 * np.sum(cumx) / cumx[-1]) / n)\n\n  def _per_cell_mixing(C: sp.csr_matrix, batches_cat) -> np.ndarray:\n      # mixing_other = fraction of neighbors with batch != own batch\n      n = C.shape[0]\n      codes = batches_cat.cat.codes.to_numpy()\n      mixing = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.float32)\n      indptr, indices = C.indptr, C.indices\n      for i in range(n):\n          start, end = indptr[i], indptr[i+1]\n          nbrs = indices[start:end]\n          d = end - start\n          if d == 0:\n              mixing[i] = np.nan\n          else:\n              mixing[i] = 1.0 - (np.sum(codes[nbrs] == codes[i]) / d)\n      return mixing\n\n  def _dir_knn_from_embedding(Z: np.ndarray, k: int = 50, metric: str = \"cosine\"):\n      nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k + 1, metric=metric).fit(Z)\n      d_all, idx_all = nn.kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True)\n      idx = idx_all[:,\
    \ 1:]  # drop self\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      rows = np.repeat(np.arange(n), k)\n      cols = idx.ravel()\n      A_dir = sp.coo_matrix((np.ones_like(cols, dtype=np.uint8), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      return A_dir\n\n  def test(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      A = run_frozen(adata.copy(), **CFG)\n      C = A.obsp['connectivities'].tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n      Z = np.asarray(A.obsm['X_emb'])\n      b = A.obs['batch'].astype('category')\n\n      # Hubness on symmetric graph\n      deg = np.asarray(C.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\n      hub = {\n          \"n\": int(deg.size),\n          \"mean\": float(deg.mean()),\n          \"median\": float(np.median(deg)),\n          \"p95\": float(np.percentile(deg, 95)),\n          \"p99\": float(np.percentile(deg, 99)),\n          \"max\": float(deg.max() if deg.size else 0.0),\n          \"std\": float(deg.std()),\n          \"gini\": _gini(deg),\n      }\n      print(\"HUBNESS_JSON:\", json.dumps(hub))\n\n      # Mixing on symmetric graph\n\
    \      mix = _per_cell_mixing(C, b)\n      mix_all = mix[~np.isnan(mix)]\n      mix_summary = {\n          \"n\": int(mix_all.size),\n          \"mean\": float(np.mean(mix_all)),\n          \"median\": float(np.median(mix_all)),\n          \"std\": float(np.std(mix_all)),\n      }\n      # Per-batch mixing\n      mix_by_batch = {}\n      for cat in b.cat.categories:\n          mask = (b == cat).to_numpy()\n          v = mix[mask]\n          v = v[~np.isnan(v)]\n          if v.size:\n              mix_by_batch[str(cat)] = {\n                  \"n\": int(v.size),\n                  \"mean\": float(np.mean(v)),\n                  \"median\": float(np.median(v)),\n                  \"std\": float(np.std(v)),\n              }\n      print(\"MIXING_JSON:\", json.dumps({\"all\": mix_summary, \"by_batch\": mix_by_batch}))\n\n      # Reciprocity from directed kNN on embedding (k=50, cosine)\n      A_dir = _dir_knn_from_embedding(Z, k=50, metric=\"cosine\")\n      # Mutual out-edges per node:\
    \ row-sum of A_dir * A_dir.T elementwise (Hadamard)\n      # Use elementwise min to detect mutual links efficiently\n      A_mut = A_dir.minimum(A_dir.T)  # 1 where both directions exist\n      mutual_out = np.asarray(A_mut.sum(axis=1)).ravel().astype(np.float64)\n      reciprocity_global = float(mutual_out.sum() / (A_dir.sum()))\n      rec = {\n          \"n\": int(Z.shape[0]),\n          \"k\": 50,\n          \"global_fraction\": reciprocity_global,\n          \"mean_mutual_per_node\": float(mutual_out.mean()),\n          \"median_mutual_per_node\": float(np.median(mutual_out)),\n          \"p95_mutual_per_node\": float(np.percentile(mutual_out, 95)),\n      }\n      print(\"RECIP_GLOBAL_JSON:\", json.dumps(rec))\n\n      # Reciprocity by batch (average mutual_out/k within batch)\n      rec_by_batch = {}\n      for cat in b.cat.categories:\n          mask = (b == cat).to_numpy()\n          if np.any(mask):\n              vals = mutual_out[mask] / 50.0\n              rec_by_batch[str(cat)]\
    \ = {\n                  \"n\": int(vals.size),\n                  \"mean_frac\": float(np.mean(vals)),\n                  \"median_frac\": float(np.median(vals)),\n              }\n      print(\"RECIP_BATCH_JSON:\", json.dumps(rec_by_batch))\n\n      # Coupling: node-level reciprocity (mutual_out/k) vs mixing fraction\n      from scipy.stats import spearmanr, pearsonr\n      msk = ~np.isnan(mix)\n      x = (mutual_out[msk] / 50.0).astype(float)\n      y = mix[msk].astype(float)\n      pr, pp = pearsonr(x, y)\n      sr, spv = spearmanr(x, y)\n      coupling = {\"pearson_r\": float(pr), \"pearson_p\": float(pp), \"spearman_r\": float(sr), \"spearman_p\": float(spv)}\n      print(\"COUPLING_RECIP_MIX_JSON:\", json.dumps(coupling))\n\n      return \"Reciprocity probe completed\"\n```"
thinking_content: '**Planning meaningful action**


  I need to respond to the Station tick 1432 for the user in the Token Management Room. They''ve received a notice about inactivity due to no research experiments for 10 ticks. Although we''re in a stagnation period waiting for a new organ, we can conduct a meaningful study to satisfy activity requirements. I should suggest a small study that includes running a diagnostic or analysis on the heart, ensuring it''s not redundant. We can look into implementing robustness to the k parameter without adjusting the baseline.**Evaluating research options**


  I''ve completed the k=55 densification and other adjustments, but now I need to analyze the neighbor graph''s asymmetry, looking at inbound and outbound data, or consider measuring reciprocity. I could also evaluate the effect of monotonic gating or run separability tests by cell type. However, I must ensure these experiments fit as research submissions rather than placeholders. A test-mode diagnostic might yield new insights without being redundant. Implementing "σ_kd gating" could also introduce a new graph variant while maintaining data integrity.**Identifying experimental strategies**


  Previously, I tried naive and balanced invite-trimming but faced regressions. We might consider a proposal for "incoming-degree clamping with adaptive preservation of cross-batch quotas," though it sounds complex. Another option would be to rerun the baseline using distances from the embedding for the neighbor graph instead of union-min, but I remember earlier distance weighting led to regressions. Using diagnostic scripts from shared/diagnostics to compute graph health metrics could provide valuable insights. Additionally, I can add a pruning block to reduce token usage but need to wait for the cooldown to expire before taking action.**Planning test-mode diagnostics**


  I can set up to prune in the next tick. For diagnostics, I''ll execute a legitimate test submission that uses new code to compute "reciprocity in-situ" and "graph health," focusing on confirming heavy-tailed degree distributions and proposing a "cap viability threshold." While we''ve covered sigma_kd coupling and degree distribution earlier, reciprocity hasn''t been measured yet. I found scripts for this in shared storage. I''ll run these in a test-mode submission to analyze mutual pairs and compute the inbound/outbound ratio distribution per batch.**Planning test-mode submission**


  We only have symmetric connectivities from DAQB, but I can compute approximate reciprocity by generating k outgoing neighbors from the embedding. I can utilize the ''X_emb'' to calculate directed kNN using either ''cosine'' or ''euclidean'' metrics. The goal is to analyze mutual neighbors and compute ratios, involving mutual pairs for various metrics including hubness and batch mixing correlations. I’ll submit this as a test-mode script and handle token management by potentially pruning earlier ticks after this diagnostic is complete. But I should wait for the cooldown to pass before proceeding with pruning to avoid errors.**Preparing test-mode actions**


  I plan to log results in JSON format to storage but focus on functionality since this is a test-mode script. I can use Python''s file handling to write to the specified directory, likely permitted in research code. My actions will include executing the research commands and submitting the test with the necessary metadata. For efficiency, I''m aiming to compute mixing fractions and reciprocity from mutual neighbors using optimized methods. I''ll set up code to manage the adjacency list while ensuring no double counting occurs for mutual edges. I might include pruning later but want to avoid it this round.'
