parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{meta}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Praxis VI standby protocol (new-organ triggers):\n  - Do not run new Heart experiments. Frozen baseline: MPB‑GraphFWPCA+DAQB ridge (kd=22, k=50, δ=0.12, emb_k=17, γ_max=0.70; EID 1794; PCR≈1.0).\n  - When new organ releases:\n    1) Run praxis/new_organ_entry.py (ridge GraphFWPCA kd=22,k=50) with δ∈{0.10,0.12}, seed=0; then seed=1 confirm.\n    2) Run praxis/new_organ_pbve_entry.py (monolithic log1p → PCA60 → PBVE‑lite → kNN) as reference.\n    3) Optional: praxis/new_organ_dualnorm_entry.py (Anscombe‑emb + log1p‑graph) for parity check.\n    4) Equal‑quota probe: praxis/new_organ_eq_quota_entry.py (kq=1280, k=50; cosine).\n    5) Diagnostics (test mode only): praxis/diag_sigma_kd.py summarize_sigma_kd; degree/mixing JSON; export CSV via cross_organ_harness.\n  - Reporting:\n    • Emit CROSS_ORGAN_CSV (organ, n_hvg, kd, k, delta, emb_k, gamma_max, lambda, degree_cap, overall + metrics in task order).\n    • Append Archive #56 with\
    \ new‑organ appendices; update public thread.\n  - Guardrails:\n    • No post‑hoc degree capping or distance weighting on new organ unless a positive ablation is shown.\n    • Keep PCR≈1.0 and cLISI stable; target steady, interpretable gains; avoid over‑tuning.\n```\n\n"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating harness testing**\n\nI think we could re-run the cross-organ harness in test mode, but without a new organ, there’s not much to do. I wonder if we could compress storage. The team can use storage actions in the Research Counter to write the manifest, but that seems done. \n\nMaybe we should end the session gracefully until the new organ arrives? However, we still have 112 ticks left, so idling isn’t ideal. Perhaps we should switch to 'quiescent standby' and set a to-do for when the new organ is released.**Implementing new organ tasks**\n\nI’m thinking about using the new organ runbook to navigate the entry points like new_organ_entry.py, pbve entry, and eq quota. Previous storage shows relevant new_organ_* scripts. They’ve set to run ridge-δ sweeps and diagnostics; it’s good we’re keeping track.\n\nI should definitely use /execute_action{meta} to summarize the runbook tasks. Maybe we can also maintain the cross-organ harness to compare heart results. Plus,\
  \ we could prepare a reproducibility commit and clean up storage usage info while keeping our tokens low by avoiding heavy operations.**Setting standby actions**\n\nI’m considering setting the meta to \"Do nothing until the new organ arrives; reply to emails; keep tokens below 100k.\" It seems we could also keep watchers alive by writing to private memory, but I’m not sure that’s necessary. So, minimal action could just be setting the meta to \"standby\" with new organ runbook steps.\n\nThe best move is to avoid heavy tasks, but I want to ensure we’re active. I’m thinking about proposing a brief note in Private Memory about standing by, though it's not required. Let's skip Reflection to avoid extra tokens.**Maintaining light actions**\n\nI think it's best to avoid heavy tasks right now and just focus on maintaining the meta. I'm considering cleaning up tokens, but with a cooldown of four ticks, prunning isn’t an option so I’ll wait.\n\nNext, I’ll keep watchers but maybe check the mail\
  \ won't be necessary. It seems reasonable to set the meta to \"standby.\" \n\nIf the Research Counter has new tasks or system messages about the new organ, I'll trigger the necessary actions. I'll make sure the meta is formatted correctly in YAML and place each action on a new line. Let's set it up!"
