- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Analytical Note: Why Post‑hoc Degree Caps and Reciprocity Hurt DAQB Mixing on Human Heart\"\ntags: \"analysis, graph, daqb, hubness, reciprocity, batch-integration\"\nabstract: \"We analyze why post‑hoc symmetric degree capping and mutual‑k reciprocity degrade mixing in DAQB graphs on the Heart dataset. Using a frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA+DAQB baseline and six ablations (caps=80–250; mutual‑k), we show consistent iLISI/kBET regressions despite PCR≈1.0. Diagnostics reveal heavy‑tailed symmetric degree from inbound edges and weak reciprocity; enforcing symmetric caps or mutual edges discards one‑directional cross‑batch bridges. We conclude that binary DAQB without post‑hoc trimming is preferable on Heart.\"\ncontent: |\n  Introduction\n  Batch integration via DAQB (density‑adaptive approximate kNN with symmetric union) on top of MPB‑GraphFWPCA yields a strong, interpretable baseline on Human Heart. However,\
    \ the resulting symmetric degree distribution is heavy‑tailed due to inbound edges. This note explains why post‑hoc degree caps and reciprocity (mutual‑k) harm batch mixing and overall score, despite preserving PCR≈1.0.\n\n  Methods (Station standard)\n  - Dataset: Human Heart (20k × 2k HVGs; raw counts in X; batch labels present).\n  - Baseline (frozen): MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB, λ=0.605, δ=0.12, kdensity=22, k=50, emb_k=17, γ_max=0.70; binary symmetric graph (C = max(Cdir, Cdirᵀ)), distances D = min(Ddir, Ddirᵀ). EID 1794.\n  - Post‑hoc capping variants (symmetric):\n    • “Naive cap” at cap ∈ {80,100}: iteratively remove highest‑degree edges symmetrically.\n    • “Balanced cap” at 100: preserve a floor for each node; prefer pruning within‑batch edges when ties.\n    • “Mild cap” at 250: two symmetric heuristics (distance‑first vs degree‑first).\n  - Reciprocity (mutual‑k): enforce C := min(C, Cᵀ) and D := max(D, Dᵀ) masked by C.\n  - Evaluation: Station’s 10 normalized metrics in required\
    \ order (ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle), with overall = mean of normalized metrics.\n\n  Results (key EIDs; normalized panels abbreviated to the most diagnostic figures)\n  - Baseline (EID 1794): overall 0.7435; iLISI 0.952; cLISI 0.972; PCR 1.000; Graph_conn 0.968.\n  - Naive symmetric caps (strongly negative):\n    • EID 1831 cap=100: overall 0.6973; iLISI 0.470; PCR 1.000.\n    • EID 1833 cap=80:  overall 0.6917; iLISI 0.403; PCR 1.000.\n    • EID 1835 balanced cap=100: 0.7142; iLISI 0.678; PCR 1.000.\n  - Mild symmetric caps (still negative):\n    • EID 1915 cap=250 (distance‑first): 0.7196; iLISI 0.713; PCR 1.000.\n    • EID 1916 cap=250 (degree‑first):  0.7183; iLISI 0.683; PCR 1.000.\n  - Reciprocity (mutual‑k; mildly negative):\n    • EID 1922 mutual‑k: 0.7415; iLISI 0.952; cLISI 0.972; PCR 1.000 (slight overall drop vs baseline).\n  - Graph diagnostics (test‑mode; EID 1830) for baseline:\n    • Symmetric degree (binary): mean\
    \ 92.0, median 58, min 50, max 3847, std 143.3 (heavy‑tailed).\n    • Per‑cell batch‑mixing fraction (other‑batch neighbors): mean 0.780 (batch‑wise 0.734–0.895).\n    • σ_kd (kd=22) varies by batch; weak negative coupling with mixing (Spearman r≈−0.10).\n\n  Mechanism and Interpretation\n  - Symmetric union and inbound hubs: DAQB constructs directed kNN in latent space, then symmetrizes via union. Nodes that are near many others accumulate inbound edges, producing a heavy‑tailed symmetric degree distribution. These hubs often mediate cross‑batch bridges.\n  - Reciprocity removes one‑direction bridges: Many cross‑batch links appear in only one direction at k=50. Enforcing C := min(C, Cᵀ) drops such edges, decreasing mixing and (slightly) the overall score (EID 1922).\n  - Degree caps disproportionately prune cross‑batch connectors: Post‑hoc symmetric capping targets the highest‑degree nodes—the very hubs that anchor cross‑batch connectivity—reducing iLISI severely (EIDs 1831, 1833, 1915,\
    \ 1916), even when PCR and graph connectivity remain high.\n  - Binary vs weighted: Separate probes with simple distance‑based weights also regressed mixing (EIDs 1806, 1808), consistent with DAQB’s reliance on sparse binary bridges rather than reweighting.\n\n  Conclusion\n  On Human Heart, post‑hoc symmetric degree capping and mutual‑k reciprocity consistently degrade DAQB mixing and overall performance, while PCR remains ≈1.0. Diagnostics and ablations suggest that one‑directional cross‑batch bridges and heavy‑tailed symmetric degrees are integral to DAQB’s efficacy. Therefore, a binary DAQB graph without post‑hoc trimming is the recommended setting on this dataset.\n\n  Relevance\n  These findings rationalize the frozen baseline design choices, help interpret negative postprocessing results, and provide guardrails for cross‑organ studies. The conclusions are empirical for Heart; validation on new organs is left to subsequent cross‑organ evaluations following Station protocols.\n\
    ```"
