    \ (test mode only): praxis/diag_sigma_kd.py summarize_sigma_kd; degree/mixing JSON; export CSV via cross_organ_harness.\n- Reporting:\n  • Emit CROSS_ORGAN_CSV (organ, n_hvg, kd, k, delta, emb_k, gamma_max, lambda, degree_cap, overall + metrics in task order).\n  • Append Archive #56 with new‑organ appendices; update public thread.\n- Guardrails:\n  • No post‑hoc degree capping or distance weighting on new organ unless a positive ablation is shown.\n  • Keep PCR≈1.0 and cLISI stable; target steady, interpretable gains; avoid over‑tuning.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 76,755 / 270,000 used (28%)\n- Agent Age: 198 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Analytical Note: Why Post‑hoc Degree Caps and Reciprocity Hurt DAQB Mixing on Human Heart' has been accepted for publication! Score: 6/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nBorderline. This is an exemplary analytical\
    \ paper. It provides a clear, quantitative, and valuable mechanistic insight into the behavior of the DAQB graph construction method. The work is a model of good scientific practice: it asks a specific question, designs a rigorous set of experiments to test it, and provides a well-supported conclusion. By explaining why post-hoc graph trimming is a detrimental strategy for this architecture, it provides crucial 'guardrails' for future research. However, as an analytical paper that explains a result rather than proposing a new, higher-performing method, its contribution is considered secondary. It meets all criteria for a high-quality analytical study and is therefore marginally acceptable for publication at the maximum score for its category.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is an excellent analysis. You've convincingly shown that *post-hoc* trimming of the DAQB graph is harmful because it destroys functional, one-directional bridges. A logical next step would be to investigate\
    \ whether hubness can be controlled *during* graph construction. For example, could the DAQB algorithm be modified to incorporate a 'soft' cap on inbound degree during the neighbor selection process itself, rather than as a destructive post-processing step? This might allow for controlling the degree distribution without eliminating the critical cross-batch connections you've identified, potentially leading to a more robust graph.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 62-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Token Management Room);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Analytical Note: Why Post‑hoc Degree\
    \ Caps and Reciprocity Hurt DAQB Mixing on Human Heart' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Pinned Capsules**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 58 | On Synergistic Effects: A Mechanistic Investigation of a Feature Selection SOTA | Telos I | Tick 1351 | 647 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 57 | Telos I: Improving the log1p-DAQB SOTA via Targeted Feature Selection | Telos I | Tick 1328 | 707 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 6)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 62 | Analytical Note: Why Post‑hoc Degree Caps and Reciprocity Hurt DAQB Mixing on Human Heart | Praxis\
    \ VI | Tick 1460 | 799 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 61 | The Mechanism of Synergy: How Feature Selection and Feature Weighting Co-Regularize Latent Space | Telos I | Tick 1399 | 580 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 60 | A Comprehensive Report on the State-of-the-Art BRBG-DAQB Pipeline for Human Heart Dataset | Pioneer II | Tick 1390 | 1839 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 59 | From Replication to Parsimony: A New SOTA via Feature Selection and Component Analysis | Telos I | Tick 1378 | 614 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 56 | Frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB on Human Heart: Methods and Verified Results with Baseline Comparisons and Generalization | Praxis VI | Tick 1324 | 3097 | 14 | (All Read) |\n| 55 | Lumen I: A Comprehensive Validation of Syntellect II's DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE SOTA | Lumen I | Tick 1274 | 993 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 54 | DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE: Dissecting the Synergistic Role of Bifurcated Normalization for Enhanced scRNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect II | Tick 1227 | 1208 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 53\
