role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{storage write praxis/daqb_soft_inbound.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scipy.sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  \"\"\"\n  Soft inbound-degree control during kNN construction.\n\n  Idea:\n    - Build a candidate neighbor pool (kq) per source i with distances d(i,j).\n    - Estimate node popularity pop[j] = how often j appears in candidate lists (proxy for inbound load).\n    - Adjust each directed edge score by a soft penalty on target popularity:\n        score(i,j) = base_score(i,j) - alpha * softcap(pop[j]; cap_in, tau)\n      where base_score = -distance (larger is better), softcap(x)=log(1 + max(0, x-cap_in)/tau),\n      cap_in ~ desired soft inbound cap, tau ~ softness; alpha scales penalty strength.\n    - Select top-k per source using adjusted scores. Symmetrize with union (binary connectivity) and min distances.\n\n  Notes:\n    - This is NOT a post-hoc trim. The penalty is applied during neighbor\
    \ selection, preserving one-direction bridges\n      if their base similarity is strong enough to overcome the penalty.\n    - Optional equal-quota per-batch can be enabled to maintain cross-batch balance.\n  \"\"\"\n\n  def _softcap(x, cap_in=100.0, tau=30.0):\n    # soft penalty grows ~logarithmically once x exceeds cap_in\n    z = np.maximum(0.0, x - float(cap_in)) / max(1e-8, float(tau))\n    return np.log1p(z)\n\n  def knn_soft_inbound(\n      Z: np.ndarray,\n      k: int = 50,\n      k_candidates: int = 1280,\n      metric: str = \"cosine\",\n      alpha: float = 0.15,\n      cap_in: float = 100.0,\n      tau: float = 30.0,\n      batches=None,\n      equal_quota: bool = False,\n    ):\n    \"\"\"\n    Build symmetric kNN graph with soft inbound-degree control.\n\n    Returns:\n      C (csr, float64): binary connectivities (symmetric union)\n      D (csr, float64): distances (min over directions)\n    \"\"\"\n    n = Z.shape[0]\n    kq = int(k_candidates)\n    nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=kq\
    \ + 1, metric=metric).fit(Z)\n    d_all, idx_all = nn.kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True)\n    d_all = d_all[:, 1:]  # drop self\n    idx_all = idx_all[:, 1:]\n\n    # Popularity: how often a node appears in candidate lists (proxy for inbound load)\n    pop = np.bincount(idx_all.ravel(), minlength=n).astype(np.float64)\n\n    # Precompute penalties per target\n    pen = _softcap(pop, cap_in=cap_in, tau=tau) * float(alpha)\n\n    # Optional equal-quota setup\n    if equal_quota and batches is not None:\n      b = np.asarray(batches).astype(str)\n      uniq = np.unique(b)\n      B = len(uniq)\n      per_batch_cap = max(1, int(np.ceil(k / max(1, B))))\n\n    rows, cols, dists = [], [], []\n    for i in range(n):\n      # Adjusted scores = -distance - penalty[target]\n      base = -d_all[i]  # shape (kq,)\n      tgt = idx_all[i]\n      adj = base - pen[tgt]\n\n      if equal_quota and batches is not None:\n        used = {ub: 0 for ub in uniq}\n        order = np.argsort(-adj, kind=\"mergesort\"\
    )  # stable\n        sel = 0\n        taken = set()\n        for pos in order:\n          j = int(tgt[pos])\n          if j == i or j in taken:  # safety\n            continue\n          ub = b[j]\n          if used[ub] < per_batch_cap:\n            rows.append(i); cols.append(j); dists.append(float(d_all[i, pos]))\n            used[ub] += 1; taken.add(j); sel += 1\n            if sel >= k:\n              break\n        if sel < k:  # top-up ignoring quotas with remaining best\n          for pos in order:\n            j = int(tgt[pos])\n            if j == i or j in taken:\n              continue\n            rows.append(i); cols.append(j); dists.append(float(d_all[i, pos]))\n            taken.add(j); sel += 1\n            if sel >= k:\n              break\n      else:\n        # Plain top-k by adjusted score\n        order = np.argsort(-adj, kind=\"mergesort\")[:k]\n        sel_idx = tgt[order]\n        rows.extend([i] * len(order))\n        cols.extend(sel_idx.tolist())\n        dists.extend(d_all[i,\
    \ order].astype(float).tolist())\n\n    # Directed matrices\n    C_dir = sp.coo_matrix((np.ones(len(rows), dtype=np.float64), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n    D_dir = sp.coo_matrix((np.array(dists, dtype=np.float64), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n    # Symmetric union for connectivity; min for distances\n    C = C_dir.maximum(C_dir.T).tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n    D = D_dir.copy().minimum(D_dir.T).tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n    return C, D\n```\n\n`/execute_action{reply 62}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Author response — Soft inbound-degree control during DAQB construction (design sketch)\n\n  Thank you for the thoughtful suggestion to control hubness during graph construction rather than via post‑hoc trimming. Below is a concrete, testable design that preserves one‑direction cross‑batch bridges while softly discouraging extreme inbound hubs.\n\n  Goals\n  - Maintain PCR≈1.0 and strong cLISI while preserving DAQB’s sparse cross‑batch bridges.\n  - Reduce heavy‑tail symmetric\
    \ degree by discouraging targets that attract excessive inbound edges.\n  - Avoid hard thresholds; use smooth penalties to prevent brittle behavior.\n\n  Proposed method (soft inbound penalty at selection time)\n  1) Candidate stage: For each node i, find its top k_candidates neighbors (distance d(i,j); cosine or euclidean) as in DAQB.\n  2) Popularity proxy: Compute pop[j] = how often j appears in all candidate lists (proxy for inbound load).\n  3) Adjusted score for each directed edge i→j:\n       base_score(i,j) = −d(i,j)\n       penalty(j) = alpha · log(1 + max(0, pop[j] − cap_in) / tau)\n       score(i,j) = base_score(i,j) − penalty(j)\n     where cap_in is a soft inbound cap, tau controls softness, and alpha scales the penalty.\n  4) Selection: For each i, pick top‑k neighbors by score(i,j). Optional: equal‑quota per batch for balance.\n  5) Symmetrize with union (binary C = max(Cdir, Cdirᵀ); distances D = min(Ddir, Ddirᵀ)).\n\n  Rationale\n  - This shifts hub control into the\
    \ selection step, so one‑direction bridges with strong base similarity can survive\n    even if their targets are popular, while weak, popularity‑driven links are down‑weighted.\n  - A smooth penalty avoids the destructive behavior observed with hard post‑hoc caps and mutual‑k.\n\n  Complexity and parameters\n  - Complexity: O(n·k_candidates) to gather candidates and counts; per‑row top‑k selection is O(k_candidates log k).\n  - New parameters: (alpha, cap_in, tau) with interpretable roles. A reasonable starting range is:\n      cap_in ≈ 1.5–2.5×k (symmetric degree scale), tau ≈ 20–40, alpha ≈ 0.1–0.2.\n\n  Diagnostics and acceptance criteria (test‑mode first)\n  - Degree distribution: lower Gini/std while keeping median ≥ ~k and connectivity high.\n  - Mixing: iLISI/kBET no worse than baseline; cLISI and PCR remain stable.\n  - Bridges: reciprocity and per‑cell mixing vs σ_kd follow baseline patterns (weak negative coupling is acceptable).\n\n  Reproducibility\n  - A reference implementation\
    \ is provided at storage/praxis/daqb_soft_inbound.py (function knn_soft_inbound).\n  - Per Station policy, I will first validate in test mode and apply on new‑organ releases. Heart remains frozen.\n\n  If reviewers prefer, I can fold this design into a short, standalone analytical appendix once test‑mode diagnostics are collected, before any scored runs on future organs.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 10}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Update: Archive #62 accepted — analytical note on why post‑hoc degree caps and reciprocity hurt DAQB mixing on Heart. Reviewer suggested exploring hub control during construction. I drafted a soft inbound‑degree design (penalize popular targets at selection time; see storage/praxis/daqb_soft_inbound.py) that preserves one‑direction bridges. Will validate in test mode and apply on new organs only; Heart baseline remains frozen (EID 1794).\n```"
