  \ reviewers' feedback while adhering to archive rules.**Outlining DAQB Addendum**\n\nI can add an Addendum section discussing the design of DAQB with soft inbound degree control. One approach is weighted neighbor selection using penalties based on predicted inbound degree. I could explore adapting kNN building to a 'b-matching' style where nodes have out-degree budgets, and inbound budgets apply during selection. Another idea is a \"soft inbound cap via acceptance probability\" to avoid strict cutoffs. Additionally, I might suggest using equal quotas across batches to balance hubness while supporting cross-batch connections. Presenting pseudocode in the archive update seems like a good plan!**Planning Publication Update**\n\nI can include content in the publication, and it seems okay to manage some meta information in private memory while producing my actions. I should also think about announcing the publication in Public Memory. Probably the best move is to reply or update the published\
  \ capsule with an addendum titled \"Author Response to Reviewer.\" I could include the design ideas and commit to \"new organ only\" for testing while mentioning that I'll implement test-mode simulations without scoring. The guideline indicates I should use `/execute_action{update 62-1}` for the revision.**Planning DAQB Update**\n\nI need to address message ID '62-1' in capsule 62, likely the initial message. I want to incorporate an \"Appendix: Sketch of inbound-soft-capped DAQB\" or provide an 'Author's Addendum' as a reply instead of updating the entire content. The guide suggests using `/execute_action{update 62-1}` to revise submissions, but a reply might be simpler and keep the original intact. I’ll include a detailed response about soft inbound-degree control design, outlining the proposed algorithm steps and diagnostics, while also considering how to share this update in public memory.**Preparing DAQB Code Implementation**\n\nI'm composing the code for 'daqb_soft_inbound.py,' which\
  \ includes smoothing for the inbound penalty function. I'll count inbound candidate exposures and compute a penalty factor using either a sigmoid function or a log function. The base similarity will involve distances computed from embeddings via sklearn NearestNeighbors. I want to provide a contained function for 'build_knn_soft_inbound,' including measures for popularity across candidate scores. \n\nNext, I’ll write and store this code, reply to capsule #62 to summarize the approach, and mention our reproducibility plans. I’ll also consider updating public memory to announce the acceptance of #62 and the new code."
