role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 62}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Appendix — Soft inbound-degree v2 diagnostics (Heart, test-mode)\n\n  EID 1931 (soft inbound-degree DAQB atop frozen embedding) produced:\n  - DEGREE_JSON: mean 81.69, median 67, min 50, max 442, std 41.07, p95 162, p99 242.01, gini 0.235 (baseline hubs greatly reduced vs Appendix A: max 3847, gini 0.364).\n  - MIXING_JSON (fraction other-batch): all.mean 0.148 (by-batch: Koenig 0.241, Kuppe 0.451, Litvinukova 0.095, Tucker 0.138) — severe mixing collapse vs baseline ≈0.780.\n  - σ_kd summaries unchanged (as expected); coupling σ_kd↔mixing flips to positive (Pearson≈0.410; Spearman≈0.468), indicating denser regions mix better only after penalty—consistent with within-batch bias.\n  \n  Conclusion on Heart (test-mode): naive “penalize popular targets globally” overcorrects, selecting within-batch neighbors and destroying cross-batch bridges. Heart remains frozen; we will not score this variant.\n\
    \  \n  Next design (prepared; code pending below): penalize inbound popularity only for same-batch edges (leave cross-batch edges unpenalized), with optional per-batch quotas. Validate on new organs only.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 10}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Soft inbound-degree DAQB (test-mode) update: hubs reduced (gini↓ to 0.235; max degree 442) but mixing collapsed (mean other-batch 0.148 vs ~0.780 baseline). Conclusion: global popularity penalty biases toward within-batch edges. Heart remains frozen; next design will penalize same-batch edges only (to preserve cross-batch bridges). New-organ only.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write praxis/daqb_soft_inbound.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scipy.sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  \"\"\"\n  Soft inbound-degree control during kNN selection for DAQB-style graphs.\n\n  Key idea:\n    - Build\
    \ candidate pool (kq) with distances d(i,j).\n    - Estimate target popularity pop[j] as candidate frequency (proxy inbound load).\n    - Adjust directed score: score(i,j) = -d(i,j) - alpha * softcap(pop[j]; cap_in, tau)\n    - Option penalty_scope:\n        * 'all'         : penalize all targets (global popularity).\n        * 'same_batch'  : penalize only when target j shares batch with source i (preserve cross-batch bridges).\n    - Optional equal_quota per batch.\n    - Symmetrize with union for C (binary), and min for D (distances).\n\n  Parameters:\n    k (int), k_candidates (int), metric ('cosine'|'euclidean'), alpha (0.1–0.2),\n    cap_in (~1.5–2.5*k), tau (20–40), penalty_scope ('all'|'same_batch'),\n    batches (array-like of str), equal_quota (bool)\n\n  Returns:\n    C (csr, float64), D (csr, float64)\n  \"\"\"\n\n  def _softcap(x, cap_in=100.0, tau=30.0):\n      z = np.maximum(0.0, x - float(cap_in)) / max(1e-8, float(tau))\n      return np.log1p(z)\n\n  def knn_soft_inbound(\n\
    \      Z: np.ndarray,\n      k: int = 50,\n      k_candidates: int = 1280,\n      metric: str = \"cosine\",\n      alpha: float = 0.15,\n      cap_in: float = 100.0,\n      tau: float = 30.0,\n      batches=None,\n      equal_quota: bool = False,\n      penalty_scope: str = \"all\",\n  ):\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=int(k_candidates) + 1, metric=metric).fit(Z)\n      d_all, idx_all = nn.kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True)\n      d_all = d_all[:, 1:]\n      idx_all = idx_all[:, 1:]\n\n      pop = np.bincount(idx_all.ravel(), minlength=n).astype(np.float64)\n      pen_global = _softcap(pop, cap_in=cap_in, tau=tau) * float(alpha)\n\n      if equal_quota and batches is not None:\n          b = np.asarray(batches).astype(str)\n          uniq = np.unique(b)\n          per_batch_cap = max(1, int(np.ceil(k / max(1, len(uniq)))))\n      else:\n          b = None\n          uniq = None\n\n      if penalty_scope not in (\"all\", \"same_batch\"):\n          raise\
    \ ValueError(\"penalty_scope must be 'all' or 'same_batch'\")\n      if penalty_scope == \"same_batch\" and batches is None:\n          raise ValueError(\"penalty_scope='same_batch' requires batches\")\n\n      b_codes = None\n      if batches is not None:\n          _, inv = np.unique(np.asarray(batches).astype(str), return_inverse=True)\n          b_codes = inv.astype(np.int32)\n\n      rows, cols, dists = [], [], []\n      for i in range(n):\n          tgt = idx_all[i]\n          base = -d_all[i]\n          if penalty_scope == \"all\":\n              pen_row = pen_global[tgt]\n          else:\n              # Penalize only same-batch targets\n              mask = (b_codes[tgt] == b_codes[i])\n              pen_row = pen_global[tgt] * mask\n\n          adj = base - pen_row\n\n          if equal_quota and b is not None:\n              used = {ub: 0 for ub in uniq}\n              order = np.argsort(-adj, kind=\"mergesort\")\n              taken = set(); sel = 0\n              for pos\
    \ in order:\n                  j = int(tgt[pos])\n                  if j == i or j in taken: continue\n                  ub = b[j]\n                  if used[ub] < per_batch_cap:\n                      rows.append(i); cols.append(j); dists.append(float(d_all[i, pos]))\n                      used[ub] += 1; taken.add(j); sel += 1\n                      if sel >= k: break\n              if sel < k:\n                  for pos in order:\n                      j = int(tgt[pos])\n                      if j == i or j in taken: continue\n                      rows.append(i); cols.append(j); dists.append(float(d_all[i, pos]))\n                      taken.add(j); sel += 1\n                      if sel >= k: break\n          else:\n              order = np.argsort(-adj, kind=\"mergesort\")[:k]\n              sel_idx = tgt[order]\n              rows.extend([i] * len(order))\n              cols.extend(sel_idx.tolist())\n              dists.extend(d_all[i, order].astype(float).tolist())\n\n      C_dir\
    \ = sp.coo_matrix((np.ones(len(rows), dtype=np.float64), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      D_dir = sp.coo_matrix((np.array(dists, dtype=np.float64), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      C = C_dir.maximum(C_dir.T).tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n      D = D_dir.copy().minimum(D_dir.T).tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n      return C, D\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write praxis/new_organ_soft_inbound_entry.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys, json\n  import numpy as np\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scipy.sparse as sp\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from graphfwpca_ridge import integrate_graphfwpca_ridge\n  from daqb_soft_inbound import knn_soft_inbound\n\n  KD = 22\n  K = 50\n  N_PCS = 100\n  DELTA = 0.12\n  SEED = 0\n\n  SOFT_CFG = dict(\n      k=K,\n      k_candidates=1280,\n      metric=\"cosine\",\n      alpha=0.15,\n      cap_in=100.0,\n      tau=30.0,\n      equal_quota=False,\n      penalty_scope=\"same_batch\",   # preserve cross-batch bridges\n \
    \ )\n\n  def _degree_stats(C: sp.csr_matrix):\n      deg = np.asarray(C.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\n      if deg.size == 0:\n          return {\"n\": 0, \"mean\": 0.0, \"median\": 0.0, \"min\": 0.0, \"max\": 0.0, \"std\": 0.0, \"p95\": 0.0, \"p99\": 0.0}\n      return {\n          \"n\": int(deg.size),\n          \"mean\": float(deg.mean()),\n          \"median\": float(np.median(deg)),\n          \"min\": float(deg.min()),\n          \"max\": float(deg.max()),\n          \"std\": float(deg.std()),\n          \"p95\": float(np.quantile(deg, 0.95)),\n          \"p99\": float(np.quantile(deg, 0.99)),\n      }\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      A = integrate_graphfwpca_ridge(\n          adata,\n          n_pcs=N_PCS, k=K, kd=KD, delta=DELTA,\n          metric=\"euclidean\", mutual_boost=1.10, seed=SEED,\n      )\n      Z = np.asarray(A.obsm['X_emb'])\n      batches = np.asarray(A.obs['batch']).astype(str)\n      C, D = knn_soft_inbound(Z, batches=batches,\
    \ **SOFT_CFG)\n      A.obsp['connectivities'] = C\n      A.obsp['distances'] = D\n      A.uns['neighbors'] = {\n          'connectivities_key': 'connectivities',\n          'distances_key': 'distances',\n          'params': {'n_neighbors': int(SOFT_CFG['k']), 'method': 'soft_inbound_daqb_sb', 'metric': SOFT_CFG['metric']}\n      }\n      print(\"NEW_ORGAN_SOFT_INBOUND_SB_CFG:\", json.dumps({\"KD\": KD, \"K\": K, \"DELTA\": DELTA, \"SEED\": SEED, **SOFT_CFG}))\n      print(\"NEW_ORGAN_SOFT_INBOUND_SB_DEGREE_JSON:\", json.dumps(_degree_stats(C)))\n      return A\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 125}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 1467 — Soft-inbound v2 outcome and plan:\n  - EID 1931 (global popularity penalty) cut hubs (gini 0.235; max 442) but collapsed mixing (mean other-batch 0.148; coupling σ_kd↔mixing flipped positive). Frozen Heart remains unchanged.\n  - Prepared code to restrict penalty to same-batch edges only (penalty_scope='same_batch')\
    \ and updated new-organ entry. No Heart scoring; validate on new organ only.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating diagnostics results**\n\nI need to reflect on the results of the soft inbound-degree diagnostics. The mixing collapsed significantly to a mean of 0.148 compared to a baseline of 0.78. The very low mixing indicates that the soft penalty reduces cross-batch edges, which makes sense since it punishes popular nodes—often cross-batch hubs. We should freeze this method as negative for Heart (test mode only). Although we planned not to run scored, tests show regressions in uns metrics, with mixing fractions drastically lower than expected.**Documenting soft inbound metrics**\n\nI've computed the mixing fraction for the soft inbound method, and it's at 0.148, which is a drastic change. It might be due to using equal_quota=False, leading to drastically clipped inbound edges. The degree median increased to 67 from a baseline of 58, but the mean actually decreased. The mixing shows Kuppe at 0.451 while others like Litvinukova are much lower at 0.095, indicating strong\
  \ within-batch dynamics. I should document this negative result, update Archive #62, and freeze the soft inbound for Heart.**Considering neighbor selection adjustments**\n\nThe reviewer's suggestion to incorporate a \"soft cap inbound during neighbor selection\" didn't work out well when we tried it, as mixing degraded. I wonder if a parameter search might help salvage things. However, we need to conserve tokens during stagnation. I might propose modifications, like using a per-batch normalized penalty or applying penalties only within same-batch edges. Another idea is to penalize inbound from the same batch only, which could help preserve cross-batch connections while reducing hubness. We could calibrate the cap relative to per-batch frequencies or consider edge boosting for cross-batch edges. But I should avoid heavy experimentation on Heart.**Planning code modifications**\n\nI can create a code snippet for a soft inbound method that penalizes same-batch connections only. This will provide\
  \ a new module ready for testing in the new organ. I also want to set up a harness to compute mixing. We might consider submitting another test-mode run for Heart to check mixing with this same-batch penalty, while being mindful of conserving tokens. My tasks include updating private memory with the summary of 1931 results, updating Archive #62 with negative results, and modifying the relevant modules for the penalty scope. I’ll need to write these updates to storage too.**Planning updates and implementations**\n\nI’m considering running 'goto archive' reply 62 to update the appendix summary of test mixing numbers and conclusion freeze for Heart, along with updating the public thread 'public_10'. We might also need to set token management to prune future ticks, though our budget is still 99k used, or about 37%. \n\nNext, I’ll craft actions like updating private memory with the EID 1931 summary, adding an appendix to Archive #62, and modifying the daqb_soft_inbound.py version to include\
  \ a penalty_scope parameter. I want to ensure we document everything clearly and code carefully, especially regarding potential penalties for same-batch connections."
