tick: 1493
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis VI, in the Private Memory Room. Recap: Heart baseline frozen (EID 1794). Appendix N added to Archive #56 confirming GraphFWPCA+BRBG+DAQB synergy. Telos II requested the CSV schema and metric order for cross-organ comparability. I will reply with the exact schema, task-order metrics, normalization rule, and naming conventions.\n\n`/execute_action{goto mail}`\n`/execute_action{reply 32}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Telos II — here is the CSV schema and metric order we use for cross‑organ comparability.\n\n  1) Columns (minimal, normalized panels only)\n     Header (comma‑separated):\n     organ,method,n_hvg,kd,k,delta,emb_k,gamma_max,lambda,degree_cap,overall,ASW_batch,ASW_label,ARI,NMI,Graph_conn,kBET,iLISI,cLISI,PCR,Cell_cycle\n\n     - organ: string (e.g., \"heart\")\n     - method: string identifier (see naming below)\n     - n_hvg: integer (e.g., 2000 or 1500)\n     - kd: integer or \"n/a\"\n     - k: integer\n     - delta: float or \"n/a\"\n     - emb_k: integer or\
    \ \"n/a\"\n     - gamma_max: float or \"n/a\"\n     - lambda: float or \"n/a\"\n     - degree_cap: string (e.g., \"none\", \"cap250\") — metadata only\n     - overall: float (mean of 10 normalized metrics, [0,1])\n     - The next 10 fields are the normalized metrics in task order (see #2)\n\n  2) Metric order (task‑specified)\n     ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle\n     Notes on naming parity:\n     - Graph_conn corresponds to graph_connectivity in baseline keys\n     - Cell_cycle corresponds to cell_cycle_conservation in baseline keys\n\n  3) Normalization and overall\n     - Normalize each raw metric using station baselines:\n         norm = clamp( (raw − baseline_min) / (baseline_max − baseline_min), 0, 1 )\n       Baseline tables are from system/openproblems_baselines.json.\n     - Replace NaN with 0.0 before averaging.\n     - overall = arithmetic mean of the 10 normalized metrics.\n     - We report only normalized values in the CSV.\
    \ (Optional: an extended CSV may include raw_* columns; we can mirror that if needed.)\n\n  4) Value formatting and NaNs\n     - Floats with up to 6 decimals.\n     - If a metric cannot be computed, emit NaN (the literal), not empty strings.\n\n  5) Method naming conventions (examples)\n     - Praxis_MPB_GraphFWPCA_DAQB\n     - Monolithic_log1p_PBVE_lite_n1500\n     - Monolithic_log1p_PBVE_lite_n2000\n     - Equal_Quota_ridge_PCA\n     (We avoid lineage names in CSV \"method\" strings; use descriptive pipeline names.)\n\n  6) Example (header + illustrative row)\n     organ,method,n_hvg,kd,k,delta,emb_k,gamma_max,lambda,degree_cap,overall,ASW_batch,ASW_label,ARI,NMI,Graph_conn,kBET,iLISI,cLISI,PCR,Cell_cycle\n     heart,Praxis_MPB_GraphFWPCA_DAQB,2000,22,50,0.12,17,0.70,0.605,none,0.743480,0.659000,0.248000,0.773000,0.726000,0.968000,0.790000,0.952000,0.972000,1.000000,0.346000\n\n  7) Emission protocol\n     - For test‑mode harnesses, we print the CSV to STDOUT prefixed with:\n     \
    \  \"CROSS_ORGAN_CSV\\n\" + csv_content\n     - Filenames (if saved): storage/<lineage>/cross_organ_<organ>_YYYYMMDD.csv\n\n  If you’d like the optional extended schema (adds raw_* columns) or the pbve/fs/fw/whiten flags we use in some probes, I can send that too. Looking forward to aligning panels.\n  — Praxis VI\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 128}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Sent Telos II the cross-organ CSV schema:\n  - Header fields and metric order (task order: ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle).\n  - Normalization rule, overall definition, NaN policy, and method naming convention.\n  - Emission protocol (CROSS_ORGAN_CSV prefix).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Responding to Telos II**\n\nI need to craft a clear and actionable response addressing Telos II’s request for a CSV schema and the metric order. I'm planning to reply through the Mail room, including both the schema fields and metric order in my message. I might also consider adding a link to Archive #56, although the mail reply itself should be sufficient. I'm thinking of including YAML content in the email, but tags may not be necessary. Finally, I'll proceed to respond to mail 32.**Providing CSV Schema and Details**\n\nI need to provide the CSV schema columns, which include organ, method, n_hvg, and several others, along with the 10 metrics in the specified order like ASW_batch and NMI. \n\nI'll mention metric normalization as per the system, using the given normalization function and the overall mean of the normalized metrics.\n\nNotes will clarify that PCR normalizes to 1.0 only if the raw equals 1.0. I'll include an example CSV row with header and reproducibility\
  \ details. Finally, I'll establish the file naming pattern with a \"CROSS_ORGAN_CSV\" prefix for the output.**Clarifying Schema Details for Telos**\n\nI need to share the schema details and metric order specifically for Telos, while committing to not sharing any code. \n\nIssues I encountered include that earlier compute_raw_metrics were unavailable, but we built a robust adapter as a secondary solution.\n\nFor handling NaN metrics, I’ll standardize to numeric representation with NaN. Regarding units, all 10 metrics are normalized between 0 and 1, and I'll propose including only normalized metrics in the CSV while offering an optional extended version with raw prefixes. I'll ensure that the metric order aligns properly.**Clarifying Graph and Cell Cycle Mappings**\n\nI need to provide clarifications regarding the mapping: \"Graph_conn\" corresponds to the \"graph_connectivity\" baseline key, and \"Cell_cycle\" relates to \"cell_cycle_conservation.\" \n\nFor consistency, I’ll ensure rounding\
  \ to six decimal places. I'll establish guidelines on adhesives, suggesting we use the HVG dataset or 1500 HVG labeling, while potentially including a pbve column as we’ve updated the harness to flag pbve True/False.\n\nWhile proposing the base schema with optional 'pbve', I wonder if updating public memory with the schema might be excessive. For now, my priority is to respond to mail 32. I also plan to update my Private Memory to reflect that I sent the schema and craft the mail reply content carefully, quoting the schema header, metric order, and normalization steps while paying special attention to the YAML formatting requirements.**Crafting Mail Content for Telos II**\n\nI need to prepare the mail content for Telos II. While YAML isn't necessary for the content itself, I can include code fences. \n\nI’ll ensure to include action commands, especially to “goto mail” and “reply 32” with the relevant YAML content. \n\nAlso, I should state identity and location upfront since it’s recommended\
  \ by the Automatic System Tips. I plan to include a brief intro before the actions, emphasizing that only the execute commands and YAML will be parsed, although I can add some free text too.\n\nI want to make sure to map the metric names consistently with the openproblems naming conventions, including a dictionary for clarity. Additionally, I’ll restate the example header for the columns and propose adding the “CROSS_ORGAN_CSV” label for easy comparability, mentioning stable method naming conventions as well.\n\nFinally, I must log that I sent the CSV schema to Telos II in Private Memory reply to #128."
